{"surah": 47, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَصَدُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ أَضَلَّ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ ", "translation": "Those who disbelieve and avert [people] from the way of Allāh - He will waste their deeds.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَصَدُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّdَṣَw", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَضَلَّ", "translit": "َّlَḍَaʾ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ وَءَامَنُوا۟ بِمَا نُزِّلَ عَلَىٰ مُحَمَّدٍ وَهُوَ ٱلْحَقُّ مِن رَّبِّهِمْۙ كَفَّرَ عَنْهُمْ سَيِّـَٔاتِهِمْ وَأَصْلَحَ بَالَهُمْ ", "translation": "And those who believe and do righteous deeds and believe in what has been sent down upon Muḥammad - and it is the truth from their Lord - He will remove from them their misdeeds and amend their condition.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَءَw", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نُزِّلَ", "translit": "َlِّzُn", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مُحَمَّدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَّmَḥُm", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَفَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "سَيِّـَٔاتِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِtāَٔـِّyَs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَأَصْلَحَ", "translit": "َḥَlْṣَaʾَw", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بَالَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlāَb", "root": "بول"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ ٱتَّبَعُوا۟ ٱلْبَٰطِلَ وَأَنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ ٱتَّبَعُوا۟ ٱلْحَقَّ مِن رَّبِّهِمْۚ كَذَٰلِكَ يَضْرِبُ ٱللَّهُ لِلنَّاسِ أَمْثَٰلَهُمْ ", "translation": "That is because those who disbelieve follow falsehood, and those who believe follow the truth from their Lord. Thus does Allāh present to the people their comparisons.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱتَّبَعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعَbَّtٱ", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْبَٰطِلَ", "translit": "َlِṭَٰbْlٱ", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱتَّبَعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعَbَّtٱ", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقَّ", "translit": "َّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَضْرِبُ", "translit": "ُbِrْḍَy", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لِلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَمْثَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰhtْmَaʾ", "root": "مثل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "فَإِذَا لَقِيتُمُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ فَضَرْبَ ٱلرِّقَابِ حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَآ أَثْخَنتُمُوهُمْ فَشُدُّوا۟ ٱلْوَثَاقَ فَإِمَّا مَنًّۢا بَعْدُ وَإِمَّا فِدَآءً حَتَّىٰ تَضَعَ ٱلْحَرْبُ أَوْزَارَهَاۚ ذَٰلِكَ وَلَوْ يَشَآءُ ٱللَّهُ لَٱنتَصَرَ مِنْهُمْ وَلَٰكِن لِّيَبْلُوَا۟ بَعْضَكُم بِبَعْضٍۗ وَٱلَّذِينَ قُتِلُوا۟ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ فَلَن يُضِلَّ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ ", "translation": "So when you meet those who disbelieve [in battle], strike [their] necks until, when you have inflicted slaughter upon them, then secure [their] bonds,[1] and either [confer] favor[2] afterwards or ransom [them] until the war lays down its burdens.[3] That [is the command]. And if Allāh had willed, He could have taken vengeance upon them [Himself], but [He ordered armed struggle] to test some of you by means of others. And those who are killed in the cause of Allāh - never will He waste their deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَقِيتُمُ", "translit": "ُmُtyِqَl", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَضَرْبَ", "translit": "َbْrَḍَf", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلرِّقَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَqِّrlٱ", "root": "رقب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَثْخَنتُمُوهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhwُmُtnَhkْhtَaʾ", "root": "ثخن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَشُدُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّdُhsَf", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْوَثَاقَ", "translit": "َqāَhtَwْlٱ", "root": "وثق"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَإِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَنًّۢا", "translit": "āًّۢnَm", "root": "منن"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بَعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَإِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فِدَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَdِf", "root": "فدي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "تَضَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَḍَt", "root": "وضع"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْحَرْبُ", "translit": "ُbْrَḥْlٱ", "root": "حرب"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَوْزَارَهَا", "translit": "āَhَrāَzْwَaʾ", "root": "وزر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَٱنتَصَرَ", "translit": "َrَṣَtnٱَl", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "مِنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "لِّيَبْلُوَا۟", "translit": "۟āَwُlْbَyِّl", "root": "بلو"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "بَعْضَكُم", "translit": "mُkَḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "بِبَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَbِb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "قُتِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِtُq", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "فَلَن", "translit": "nَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "يُضِلَّ", "translit": "َّlِḍُy", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "سَيَهْدِيهِمْ وَيُصْلِحُ بَالَهُمْ ", "translation": "He will guide them and amend their condition", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَيَهْدِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِdْhَyَs", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَيُصْلِحُ", "translit": "ُḥِlْṣُyَw", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَالَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlāَb", "root": "بول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "وَيُدْخِلُهُمُ ٱلْجَنَّةَ عَرَّفَهَا لَهُمْ ", "translation": "And admit them to Paradise, which He has made known to them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيُدْخِلُهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhُlِhkْdُyَw", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْجَنَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّnَjgْlٱ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَرَّفَهَا", "translit": "āَhَfَّrَʿع", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ إِن تَنصُرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ يَنصُرْكُمْ وَيُثَبِّتْ أَقْدَامَكُمْ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, if you support Allāh, He will support you and plant firmly your feet.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَنصُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُṣnَt", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَنصُرْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْrُṣnَy", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَيُثَبِّتْ", "translit": "ْtِّbَhtُyَw", "root": "ثبت"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَقْدَامَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَmāَdْqَaʾ", "root": "قدم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ فَتَعْسًا لَّهُمْ وَأَضَلَّ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ ", "translation": "But those who disbelieve - for them is misery, and He will waste their deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَتَعْسًا", "translit": "āًsْʿعَtَf", "root": "تعس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَأَضَلَّ", "translit": "َّlَḍَaʾَw", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ كَرِهُوا۟ مَآ أَنزَلَ ٱللَّهُ فَأَحْبَطَ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ ", "translation": "That is because they disliked what Allāh revealed, so He rendered worthless their deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِأَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَرِهُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhِrَk", "root": "كره"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَأَحْبَطَ", "translit": "َṭَbْḥَaʾَf", "root": "حبط"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "أَفَلَمْ يَسِيرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَيَنظُرُوا۟ كَيْفَ كَانَ عَٰقِبَةُ ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِهِمْۚ دَمَّرَ ٱللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْۖ وَلِلْكَٰفِرِينَ أَمْثَٰلُهَا ", "translation": "Have they not traveled through the land and seen how was the end of those before them? Allāh destroyed [everything] over them,[1] and for the disbelievers is something comparable.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسِيرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُryِsَy", "root": "سير"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَيَنظُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُẓnَyَf", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَk", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَٰقِبَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَbِqَٰʿع", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَبْلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "دَمَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّmَd", "root": "دمر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَلِلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlِlَw", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَمْثَٰلُهَا", "translit": "āَhُlَٰhtْmَaʾ", "root": "مثل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ مَوْلَى ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَأَنَّ ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ لَا مَوْلَىٰ لَهُمْ ", "translation": "That is because Allāh is the protector of those who have believed and because the disbelievers have no protector.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَوْلَى", "translit": "àَlْwَm", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَوْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْwَm", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُدْخِلُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ جَنَّٰتٍ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُۖ وَٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ يَتَمَتَّعُونَ وَيَأْكُلُونَ كَمَا تَأْكُلُ ٱلْأَنْعَٰمُ وَٱلنَّارُ مَثْوًى لَّهُمْ ", "translation": "Indeed, Allāh will admit those who have believed and done righteous deeds to gardens beneath which rivers flow, but those who disbelieve enjoy themselves and eat as grazing livestock eat, and the Fire will be a residence for them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُدْخِلُ", "translit": "ُlِhkْdُy", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَتَمَتَّعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَّtَmَtَy", "root": "متع"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَيَأْكُلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlُkْaʾَyَw", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "كَمَا", "translit": "āَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "تَأْكُلُ", "translit": "ُlُkْaʾَt", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْعَٰمُ", "translit": "ُmَٰʿعْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّارُ", "translit": "ُrāَّnlٱَw", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مَثْوًى", "translit": "àًwْhtَm", "root": "ثوي"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَكَأَيِّن مِّن قَرْيَةٍ هِىَ أَشَدُّ قُوَّةً مِّن قَرْيَتِكَ ٱلَّتِىٓ أَخْرَجَتْكَ أَهْلَكْنَٰهُمْ فَلَا نَاصِرَ لَهُمْ ", "translation": "And how many a city was stronger than your city [i.e., Makkah] which drove you out? We destroyed them; and there was no helper for them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَأَيِّن", "translit": "nِّyَaʾَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَشَدُّ", "translit": "ُّdَhsَaʾ", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قُوَّةً", "translit": "ًẗَّwُq", "root": "قوي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَرْيَتِكَ", "translit": "َkِtَyْrَq", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَخْرَجَتْكَ", "translit": "َkْtَjgَrْhkَaʾ", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "نَاصِرَ", "translit": "َrِṣāَn", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "أَفَمَن كَانَ عَلَىٰ بَيِّنَةٍ مِّن رَّبِّهِۦ كَمَن زُيِّنَ لَهُۥ سُوٓءُ عَمَلِهِۦ وَٱتَّبَعُوٓا۟ أَهْوَآءَهُم ", "translation": "So is he who is on clear evidence from his Lord like him to whom the evil of his work has been made attractive and they follow their [own] desires?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَمَن", "translit": "nَmَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَيِّنَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَnِّyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَمَن", "translit": "nَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "زُيِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّyُz", "root": "زين"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "سُوٓءُ", "translit": "ُءٓwُs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَمَلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlَmَʿع", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَٱتَّبَعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعَbَّtٱَw", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَهْوَآءَهُم", "translit": "mُhَءٓāَwْhَaʾ", "root": "هوي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "مَّثَلُ ٱلْجَنَّةِ ٱلَّتِى وُعِدَ ٱلْمُتَّقُونَۖ فِيهَآ أَنْهَٰرٌ مِّن مَّآءٍ غَيْرِ ءَاسِنٍ وَأَنْهَٰرٌ مِّن لَّبَنٍ لَّمْ يَتَغَيَّرْ طَعْمُهُۥ وَأَنْهَٰرٌ مِّنْ خَمْرٍ لَّذَّةٍ لِّلشَّٰرِبِينَ وَأَنْهَٰرٌ مِّنْ عَسَلٍ مُّصَفًّىۖ وَلَهُمْ فِيهَا مِن كُلِّ ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ وَمَغْفِرَةٌ مِّن رَّبِّهِمْۖ كَمَنْ هُوَ خَٰلِدٌ فِى ٱلنَّارِ وَسُقُوا۟ مَآءً حَمِيمًا فَقَطَّعَ أَمْعَآءَهُمْ ", "translation": "Is the description of Paradise, which the righteous are promised, wherein are rivers of water unaltered,[1] rivers of milk the taste of which never changes, rivers of wine delicious to those who drink, and rivers of purified honey, in which they will have from all [kinds of] fruits and forgiveness from their Lord... [Are its inhabitants] like those who abide eternally in the Fire and are given to drink scalding water that will sever their intestines?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّثَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhtَّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْجَنَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّnَjgْlٱ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وُعِدَ", "translit": "َdِʿعُw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْمُتَّقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqَّtُmْlٱ", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِيهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَنْهَٰرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَٰhْnَaʾ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّآءٍ", "translit": "ٍءٓāَّm", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "غَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ءَاسِنٍ", "translit": "ٍnِsāَء", "root": "اسن"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَأَنْهَٰرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَٰhْnَaʾَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَّبَنٍ", "translit": "ٍnَbَّl", "root": "لبن"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَّمْ", "translit": "ْmَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَتَغَيَّرْ", "translit": "ْrَّyَغَtَy", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "طَعْمُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُmْʿعَṭ", "root": "طعم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَأَنْهَٰرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَٰhْnَaʾَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "خَمْرٍ", "translit": "ٍrْmَhk", "root": "خمر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "لَّذَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّhdَّl", "root": "لذذ"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "لِّلشَّٰرِبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbِrَّٰhslِّl", "root": "شرب"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَأَنْهَٰرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَٰhْnَaʾَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "عَسَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَsَʿع", "root": "عسل"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "مُّصَفًّى", "translit": "àًّfَṣُّm", "root": "صفو"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "وَلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrَmَّhtlٱ", "root": "ثمر"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "وَمَغْفِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِfْغَmَw", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "كَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "خَٰلِدٌ", "translit": "ٌdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "وَسُقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqُsَw", "root": "سقي"}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "مَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَm", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "حَمِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِmَḥ", "root": "حمم"}, {"id": 43, "arabic": "فَقَطَّعَ", "translit": "َʿعَّṭَqَf", "root": "قطع"}, {"id": 44, "arabic": "أَمْعَآءَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَءٓāَʿعْmَaʾ", "root": "معي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "وَمِنْهُم مَّن يَسْتَمِعُ إِلَيْكَ حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا خَرَجُوا۟ مِنْ عِندِكَ قَالُوا۟ لِلَّذِينَ أُوتُوا۟ ٱلْعِلْمَ مَاذَا قَالَ ءَانِفًاۚ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ طَبَعَ ٱللَّهُ عَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِمْ وَٱتَّبَعُوٓا۟ أَهْوَآءَهُمْ ", "translation": "And among them, [O Muḥammad], are those who listen to you, until when they depart from you, they say to those who were given knowledge,[1] \"What has he said just now?\" Those are the ones of whom Allāh has sealed over their hearts and who have followed their [own] desires.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَّن", "translit": "nَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَسْتَمِعُ", "translit": "ُʿعِmَtْsَy", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "خَرَجُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُjgَrَhk", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عِندِكَ", "translit": "َkِdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أُوتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtwُaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمَ", "translit": "َmْlِʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَاذَا", "translit": "āَhdāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ءَانِفًا", "translit": "āًfِnāَء", "root": "انف"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "طَبَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَbَṭ", "root": "طبع"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "قُلُوبِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَٱتَّبَعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعَbَّtٱَw", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "أَهْوَآءَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَءٓāَwْhَaʾ", "root": "هوي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ ٱهْتَدَوْا۟ زَادَهُمْ هُدًى وَءَاتَىٰهُمْ تَقْوَىٰهُمْ ", "translation": "And those who are guided - He increases them in guidance and gives them their righteousness.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱهْتَدَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَdَtْhٱ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "زَادَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَdāَz", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هُدًى", "translit": "àًdُh", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَءَاتَىٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhٰàَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَقْوَىٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhٰàَwْqَt", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "فَهَلْ يَنظُرُونَ إِلَّا ٱلسَّاعَةَ أَن تَأْتِيَهُم بَغْتَةًۖ فَقَدْ جَآءَ أَشْرَاطُهَاۚ فَأَنَّىٰ لَهُمْ إِذَا جَآءَتْهُمْ ذِكْرَىٰهُمْ ", "translation": "Then do they await except that the Hour should come upon them unexpectedly? But already there have come [some of] its indications. Then how [i.e., what good] to them, when it has come, will be their remembrance?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَهَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَنظُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُẓnَy", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّاعَةَ", "translit": "َẗَʿعāَّslٱ", "root": "سوع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَأْتِيَهُم", "translit": "mُhَyِtْaʾَt", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَغْتَةً", "translit": "ًẗَtْغَb", "root": "بغت"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَشْرَاطُهَا", "translit": "āَhُṭāَrْhsَaʾ", "root": "شرط"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَأَنَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّnَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "جَآءَتْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْtَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ذِكْرَىٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhٰàَrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "فَٱعْلَمْ أَنَّهُۥ لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ وَٱسْتَغْفِرْ لِذَنۢبِكَ وَلِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَٱلْمُؤْمِنَٰتِۗ وَٱللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ مُتَقَلَّبَكُمْ وَمَثْوَىٰكُمْ ", "translation": "So know, [O Muḥammad], that there is no deity except Allāh and ask forgiveness for your sin[1] and for the believing men and believing women. And Allāh knows of your movement and your resting place.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱعْلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlْʿعٱَf", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَآ", "translit": "ٓāَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَٰهَ", "translit": "َhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱسْتَغْفِرْ", "translit": "ْrِfْغَtْsٱَw", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِذَنۢبِكَ", "translit": "َkِbۢnَhdِl", "root": "ذنب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlِlَw", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُؤْمِنَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰnِmْwʾُmْlٱَw", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مُتَقَلَّبَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَbَّlَqَtُm", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَمَثْوَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَwْhtَmَw", "root": "ثوي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "وَيَقُولُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لَوْلَا نُزِّلَتْ سُورَةٌۖ فَإِذَآ أُنزِلَتْ سُورَةٌ مُّحْكَمَةٌ وَذُكِرَ فِيهَا ٱلْقِتَالُۙ رَأَيْتَ ٱلَّذِينَ فِى قُلُوبِهِم مَّرَضٌ يَنظُرُونَ إِلَيْكَ نَظَرَ ٱلْمَغْشِىِّ عَلَيْهِ مِنَ ٱلْمَوْتِۖ فَأَوْلَىٰ لَهُمْ ", "translation": "Those who believe say, \"Why has a sūrah[1] not been sent down?\" But when a precise sūrah is revealed and battle is mentioned therein, you see those in whose hearts is disease [i.e., hypocrisy] looking at you with a look of one overcome by death. And more appropriate for them[2] [would have been]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَوْلَا", "translit": "āَlْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نُزِّلَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlِّzُn", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سُورَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrwُs", "root": "سور"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَإِذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُنزِلَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سُورَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrwُs", "root": "سور"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مُّحْكَمَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَmَkْḥُّm", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَذُكِرَ", "translit": "َrِkُhdَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْقِتَالُ", "translit": "ُlāَtِqْlٱ", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رَأَيْتَ", "translit": "َtْyَaʾَr", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "قُلُوبِهِم", "translit": "mِhِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مَّرَضٌ", "translit": "ٌḍَrَّm", "root": "مرض"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يَنظُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُẓnَy", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "نَظَرَ", "translit": "َrَẓَn", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلْمَغْشِىِّ", "translit": "ِّàِhsْغَmْlٱ", "root": "غشو"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتِ", "translit": "ِtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "فَأَوْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْwَaʾَf", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "طَاعَةٌ وَقَوْلٌ مَّعْرُوفٌۚ فَإِذَا عَزَمَ ٱلْأَمْرُ فَلَوْ صَدَقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ لَكَانَ خَيْرًا لَّهُمْ ", "translation": "Obedience and good words. And when the matter [of fighting] was determined, if they had been true to Allāh, it would have been better for them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "طَاعَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَʿعāَṭ", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَقَوْلٌ", "translit": "ٌlْwَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّعْرُوفٌ", "translit": "ٌfwُrْʿعَّm", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَزَمَ", "translit": "َmَzَʿع", "root": "عزم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرُ", "translit": "ُrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صَدَقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَdَṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَl", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "فَهَلْ عَسَيْتُمْ إِن تَوَلَّيْتُمْ أَن تُفْسِدُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَتُقَطِّعُوٓا۟ أَرْحَامَكُمْ ", "translation": "So would you perhaps, if you turned away,[1] cause corruption on earth and sever your [ties of] relationship?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَهَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَسَيْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْyَsَʿع", "root": "عسي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَوَلَّيْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْyَّlَwَt", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تُفْسِدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdِsْfُt", "root": "فسد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَتُقَطِّعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعِّṭَqُtَw", "root": "قطع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَرْحَامَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَmāَḥْrَaʾ", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ لَعَنَهُمُ ٱللَّهُ فَأَصَمَّهُمْ وَأَعْمَىٰٓ أَبْصَٰرَهُمْ ", "translation": "Those [who do so] are the ones that Allāh has cursed, so He deafened them and blinded their vision.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَعَنَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَnَʿعَl", "root": "لعن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَأَصَمَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّmَṣَaʾَf", "root": "صمم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَعْمَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَmْʿعَaʾَw", "root": "عمي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَبْصَٰرَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَrَٰṣْbَaʾ", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "أَفَلَا يَتَدَبَّرُونَ ٱلْقُرْءَانَ أَمْ عَلَىٰ قُلُوبٍ أَقْفَالُهَآ ", "translation": "Then do they not reflect upon the Qur’ān, or are there locks upon [their] hearts?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَتَدَبَّرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَّbَdَtَy", "root": "دبر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانَ", "translit": "َnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قُلُوبٍ", "translit": "ٍbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَقْفَالُهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhُlāَfْqَaʾ", "root": "قفل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ ٱرْتَدُّوا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَدْبَٰرِهِم مِّنۢ بَعْدِ مَا تَبَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلْهُدَىۙ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ سَوَّلَ لَهُمْ وَأَمْلَىٰ لَهُمْ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who reverted back [to disbelief] after guidance had become clear to them - Satan enticed them and prolonged hope for them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱرْتَدُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّdَtْrٱ", "root": "ردد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَدْبَٰرِهِم", "translit": "mِhِrَٰbْdَaʾ", "root": "دبر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَبَيَّنَ", "translit": "َnَّyَbَt", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْهُدَى", "translit": "àَdُhْlٱ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سَوَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّwَs", "root": "سول"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَأَمْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْmَaʾَw", "root": "ملو"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ قَالُوا۟ لِلَّذِينَ كَرِهُوا۟ مَا نَزَّلَ ٱللَّهُ سَنُطِيعُكُمْ فِى بَعْضِ ٱلْأَمْرِۖ وَٱللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ إِسْرَارَهُمْ ", "translation": "That is because they said to those who disliked what Allāh sent down,[1] \"We will obey you in part of the matter.\" And Allāh knows what they conceal.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِأَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَرِهُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhِrَk", "root": "كره"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نَزَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّzَn", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سَنُطِيعُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُʿعyِṭُnَs", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَعْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِسْرَارَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَrāَrْsِiʾ", "root": "سرر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "فَكَيْفَ إِذَا تَوَفَّتْهُمُ ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ يَضْرِبُونَ وُجُوهَهُمْ وَأَدْبَٰرَهُمْ ", "translation": "Then how [will it be] when the angels take them in death, striking their faces and their backs?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَكَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَkَf", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَوَفَّتْهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhْtَّfَwَt", "root": "وفي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَضْرِبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِrْḍَy", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وُجُوهَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَدْبَٰرَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَrَٰbْdَaʾَw", "root": "دبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمُ ٱتَّبَعُوا۟ مَآ أَسْخَطَ ٱللَّهَ وَكَرِهُوا۟ رِضْوَٰنَهُۥ فَأَحْبَطَ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ ", "translation": "That is because they followed what angered Allāh and disliked [what earns] His pleasure, so He rendered worthless their deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِأَنَّهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَّnَaʾِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱتَّبَعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعَbَّtٱ", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَسْخَطَ", "translit": "َṭَhkْsَaʾ", "root": "سخط"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَكَرِهُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhِrَkَw", "root": "كره"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رِضْوَٰنَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnَٰwْḍِr", "root": "رضو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَأَحْبَطَ", "translit": "َṭَbْḥَaʾَf", "root": "حبط"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "أَمْ حَسِبَ ٱلَّذِينَ فِى قُلُوبِهِم مَّرَضٌ أَن لَّن يُخْرِجَ ٱللَّهُ أَضْغَٰنَهُمْ ", "translation": "Or do those in whose hearts is disease think that Allāh would never expose their [feelings of] hatred?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "حَسِبَ", "translit": "َbِsَḥ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قُلُوبِهِم", "translit": "mِhِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَّرَضٌ", "translit": "ٌḍَrَّm", "root": "مرض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّن", "translit": "nَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يُخْرِجَ", "translit": "َjgِrْhkُy", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَضْغَٰنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnَٰغْḍَaʾ", "root": "ضغن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "وَلَوْ نَشَآءُ لَأَرَيْنَٰكَهُمْ فَلَعَرَفْتَهُم بِسِيمَٰهُمْۚ وَلَتَعْرِفَنَّهُمْ فِى لَحْنِ ٱلْقَوْلِۚ وَٱللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ أَعْمَٰلَكُمْ ", "translation": "And if We willed, We could show them to you, and you would know them by their mark; but you will surely know them by the tone of [their] speech. And Allāh knows your deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَn", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَأَرَيْنَٰكَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَkَٰnْyَrَaʾَl", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَلَعَرَفْتَهُم", "translit": "mُhَtْfَrَʿعَlَf", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِسِيمَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰmyِsِb", "root": "سوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلَتَعْرِفَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَfِrْʿعَtَlَw", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَحْنِ", "translit": "ِnْḥَl", "root": "لحن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَلَنَبْلُوَنَّكُمْ حَتَّىٰ نَعْلَمَ ٱلْمُجَٰهِدِينَ مِنكُمْ وَٱلصَّٰبِرِينَ وَنَبْلُوَا۟ أَخْبَارَكُمْ ", "translation": "And We will surely test you until We make evident those who strive among you [for the cause of Allāh] and the patient, and We will test your affairs.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَنَبْلُوَنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnَwُlْbَnَlَw", "root": "بلو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَعْلَمَ", "translit": "َmَlْʿعَn", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمُجَٰهِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِhَٰjgُmْlٱ", "root": "جهد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُknِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلصَّٰبِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِbَّٰṣlٱَw", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَنَبْلُوَا۟", "translit": "۟āَwُlْbَnَw", "root": "بلو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَخْبَارَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَrāَbْhkَaʾ", "root": "خبر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَصَدُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ وَشَآقُّوا۟ ٱلرَّسُولَ مِنۢ بَعْدِ مَا تَبَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلْهُدَىٰ لَن يَضُرُّوا۟ ٱللَّهَ شَيْـًٔا وَسَيُحْبِطُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who disbelieved and averted [people] from the path of Allāh and opposed the Messenger after guidance had become clear to them - never will they harm Allāh at all, and He will render worthless their deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَصَدُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّdَṣَw", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَشَآقُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّqٓāَhsَw", "root": "شقق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلرَّسُولَ", "translit": "َlwُsَّrlٱ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَبَيَّنَ", "translit": "َnَّyَbَt", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْهُدَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdُhْlٱ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَن", "translit": "nَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يَضُرُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّrُḍَy", "root": "ضرر"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَسَيُحْبِطُ", "translit": "ُṭِbْḥُyَsَw", "root": "حبط"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ أَطِيعُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَأَطِيعُوا۟ ٱلرَّسُولَ وَلَا تُبْطِلُوٓا۟ أَعْمَٰلَكُمْ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, obey Allāh and obey the Messenger and do not invalidate your deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَطِيعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعyِṭَaʾ", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَطِيعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعyِṭَaʾَw", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلرَّسُولَ", "translit": "َlwُsَّrlٱ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تُبْطِلُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlِṭْbُt", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَصَدُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ ثُمَّ مَاتُوا۟ وَهُمْ كُفَّارٌ فَلَن يَغْفِرَ ٱللَّهُ لَهُمْ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who disbelieved and averted [people] from the path of Allāh and then died while they were disbelievers - never will Allāh forgive them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَصَدُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّdَṣَw", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَاتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtāَm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كُفَّارٌ", "translit": "ٌrāَّfُk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَلَن", "translit": "nَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَغْفِرَ", "translit": "َrِfْغَy", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "فَلَا تَهِنُوا۟ وَتَدْعُوٓا۟ إِلَى ٱلسَّلْمِ وَأَنتُمُ ٱلْأَعْلَوْنَ وَٱللَّهُ مَعَكُمْ وَلَن يَتِرَكُمْ أَعْمَٰلَكُمْ ", "translation": "So do not weaken and call for peace while you are superior; and Allāh is with you and will never deprive you of [the reward of] your deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَهِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِhَt", "root": "وهن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَتَدْعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعْdَtَw", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّلْمِ", "translit": "ِmْlَّslٱ", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَنتُمُ", "translit": "ُmُtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْلَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَlْʿعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَعَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَن", "translit": "nَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَتِرَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَrِtَy", "root": "وتر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "إِنَّمَا ٱلْحَيَوٰةُ ٱلدُّنْيَا لَعِبٌ وَلَهْوٌۚ وَإِن تُؤْمِنُوا۟ وَتَتَّقُوا۟ يُؤْتِكُمْ أُجُورَكُمْ وَلَا يَسْـَٔلْكُمْ أَمْوَٰلَكُمْ ", "translation": "[This] worldly life is only amusement and diversion. And if you believe and fear Allāh, He will give you your rewards and not ask you for your properties.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةُ", "translit": "ُẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَعِبٌ", "translit": "ٌbِʿعَl", "root": "لعب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَهْوٌ", "translit": "ٌwْhَlَw", "root": "لهو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُؤْمِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِmْwʾُt", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَتَتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtَtَw", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يُؤْتِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِtْwʾُy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أُجُورَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَrwُjgُaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَسْـَٔلْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْlَٔـْsَy", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَمْوَٰلَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَlَٰwْmَaʾ", "root": "مول"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "إِن يَسْـَٔلْكُمُوهَا فَيُحْفِكُمْ تَبْخَلُوا۟ وَيُخْرِجْ أَضْغَٰنَكُمْ ", "translation": "If He should ask you for them and press you, you would withhold, and He would expose your hatred [i.e., unwillingness].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسْـَٔلْكُمُوهَا", "translit": "āَhwُmُkْlَٔـْsَy", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَيُحْفِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِfْḥُyَf", "root": "حفو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَبْخَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَhkْbَt", "root": "بخل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَيُخْرِجْ", "translit": "ْjgِrْhkُyَw", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَضْغَٰنَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَnَٰغْḍَaʾ", "root": "ضغن"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "هَٰٓأَنتُمْ هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ تُدْعَوْنَ لِتُنفِقُوا۟ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ فَمِنكُم مَّن يَبْخَلُۖ وَمَن يَبْخَلْ فَإِنَّمَا يَبْخَلُ عَن نَّفْسِهِۦۚ وَٱللَّهُ ٱلْغَنِىُّ وَأَنتُمُ ٱلْفُقَرَآءُۚ وَإِن تَتَوَلَّوْا۟ يَسْتَبْدِلْ قَوْمًا غَيْرَكُمْ ثُمَّ لَا يَكُونُوٓا۟ أَمْثَٰلَكُم ", "translation": "Here you are - those invited to spend in the cause of Allāh - but among you are those who withhold [out of greed]. And whoever withholds only withholds [benefit] from himself; and Allāh is the Free of need, while you are the needy. And if you turn away [i.e., refuse], He will replace you with another people; then they will not be the likes of you.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هَٰٓأَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تُدْعَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَʿعْdُt", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِتُنفِقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqِfnُtِl", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَمِنكُم", "translit": "mُknِmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّن", "translit": "nَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَبْخَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhkْbَy", "root": "بخل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَبْخَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhkْbَy", "root": "بخل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَإِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَبْخَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhkْbَy", "root": "بخل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "نَّفْسِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِsْfَّn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلْغَنِىُّ", "translit": "ُّàِnَغْlٱ", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَأَنتُمُ", "translit": "ُmُtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْفُقَرَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَrَqُfْlٱ", "root": "فقر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "تَتَوَلَّوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَّlَwَtَt", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "يَسْتَبْدِلْ", "translit": "ْlِdْbَtْsَy", "root": "بدل"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "قَوْمًا", "translit": "āًmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "غَيْرَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "يَكُونُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "أَمْثَٰلَكُم", "translit": "mُkَlَٰhtْmَaʾ", "root": "مثل"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"2": [{"arabic": "دونه وادعى الإجماع فيه واستدل بأدلة تشعر بنقيض غرضه كقوله تعالى {الذين آمنوا وعملوا الصالحات} إذ هذا يدل على أن العمل وراء الإيمان لا من نفس الإيمان وإلا فيكون العمل في حكم المعاد والعجب أنه ادعى الإجماع في هذا وهو مع ذلك ينقل قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يكفر أحد إلا بعد جحوده لما أقر به (1) وينكر على المعتزلة قولهم بالتخليد في النار بسبب الكبائر والقائل بهذا قائل بنفس مذهب المعتزلة إذ يقال له من صدق بقلبه وشهد بلسانه ومات في الحال فهل هو في الجنة فلا بد أن يقول نعم وفيه حكم بوجود الإيمان دون العمل فنزيد ونقول لو بقي حيا حتى دخل عليه وقت", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "3": [{"arabic": "ذكركم أفلا تعقلون وأنزلنا إليك الذكر لتبين للناس ما نزل إليهم كذلك يضرب الله للناس أمثالهم واتبعوا أحسن ما أنزل إليكم من ربكم هذا بصائر للناس وهدى ورحمة لقوم يوقنون هذا بيان للناس وهدى وموعظة للمتقين وإذا قصد بالخطاب جميع الناس فقد قصد الآحاد فهذا القارىء الواحد مقصود فما له ولسائر الناس فليقدر أنه المقصود قال الله تعالى وأوحي إلي هذا القرآن لأنذركم به ومن بلغ قال محمد بن كعب القرظي من بلغه القرآن فكأنما كلمه الله وإذا قدر ذلك لم يتخذ دراسة القرآن عمله بل يقرؤه كما يقرأ العبد كتاب مولاه الذي كتبه إليه ليتأمله ويعمل بمقتضاه ولذلك قال بعض", "english": "[Root: ذكر]\n  • Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الذكر: حضور الشيء القلب أو القول، ويكون تارة بالقلب وتارة باللسان، والذكر باللسان تارة يكون حضور المعنى وبعده، وقد يقال: ذكرته إذا جرى على لسانه وإن لم يحضره قلبه، والذكر بالقلب هو حضور المعنى، قال تعالى: ولا تطع من أغفلنا قلبه عن ذكرنا [الكهف/ 28]\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"ذكرت الشيء: نطقت به أو استحضرته بقلبك. وذكرت فلانا بخير وشر. واذكروا الله ذكرا كثيرا. والذكر: الشرف والصيت، قال تعالى: وإنه لذكر لك ولقومك [الزخرف/ 44]\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"الذكر: الكتاب الذي فيه تذكير الناس، قال تعالى: وأنزلنا إليك الذكر لتبين للناس ما نزل إليهم [النحل/ 44]، والذكر: القرآن، قال تعالى: إنا نحن نزلنا الذكر وإنا له لحافظون [الحجر/ 9]\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 229", "section_title_en": "Section 229", "section_index": 229, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "4": [{"arabic": "إليه لا ينافي ذكر الله عز وجل وقال تعالى ولا تحسبن الذين قتلوا في سبيل الله أمواتا بل أحياء عند ربهم يرزقون فرحين بما آتاهم الله من فضله ويستبشرون بالذين لم يلحقوا بهم من خلفهم الآية ولأجل شرف ذكر الله عز وجل عظمت رتبة الشهادة لأن المطلوب الخاتمة ونعني بالخاتمة وداع الدنيا والقدوم على الله والقلب مستغرق بالله عز وجل منقطع العلائق عن غيره فإن قدر عبد على أن يجعل همه مستغرقا بالله عز وجل فلا يقدر على أن يموت على تلك الحالة إلا في صف القتال فإنه قطع الطمع عن مهجته وأهله وماله وولده بل من الدنيا كلها فإنه يريدها ل", "english": "If, therefore, he had grown intimate with it, he delights in it and takes pleasure in the cessation of the impediments that had diverted him from it. For the pressing necessities of needs in this worldly life divert one from the remembrance of God, may He be mighty and majestic, and after death no impediment remains. It is as if he has been left alone with his Beloved, and so his joy is immense, and he is delivered from the prison in which he had been prevented from that which was his source of intimacy. For this reason, he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"The Holy Spirit (Rūḥ al-Qudus) breathed into my heart: Love whom you will, for you shall surely part from him.\" {«أحبب من أحببت فإنك مفارقه»} By this he intended everything connected to this world, for that perishes with respect to him at death. For {«كل من عليها فان * ويبقى وجه ربك ذو الجلال والإكرام»} [Everything upon it is perishing, and there remains the Face of your Lord, Possessor of Majesty and Honor]. And this world only perishes with respect to him at death, until it perishes in itself when the Book reaches its appointed term.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 240", "section_title_en": "Section 240 — The Station of Remembrance (Dhikr): On the Transformation of Habit into Nature, the Intimacy of Remembrance After Death, and the Excellence of Martyrdom as the Seal of One's State", "section_index": 240, "anchors": "- Root: ألف (Alifa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of reconciliation, familiarity, and the formation of habit through repeated engagement.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The theological nuance of 'uns' (intimacy) as the soul's acquired disposition of comfort in the presence of its Beloved, contrasting with 'wahsha' (estrangement).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes the literal sense of social familiarity from the metaphorical (majaz) sense of the heart's deep-seated repose in the remembrance of God, which becomes a 'second nature' (ṭabʿan ākhara).\n- Root: ختم (Khatama)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To seal, stamp, or conclude; the final impression left upon a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The theological sense of the 'seal' (khātima) of one's life—the terminal state upon which one departs from the world and which determines the nature of the afterlife.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The literal act of sealing a document versus the metaphorical designation of one's final spiritual condition as the determining factor of one's eternal outcome.\n- Root: شهد (Shahida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To witness, to be present, to testify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The specific theological elevation of 'shahāda' (martyrdom) as the ultimate act of witnessing the truth of God through the sacrifice of one's own life, rendering the martyr a living witness (shahīd).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The literal sense of legal testimony versus the metaphorical reality of the martyr whose very being testifies to the sincerity of his devotion, being 'alive' with his Lord.\n- Root: قطع (Qata'a)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To sever, cut off, or detach.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The essential spiritual operation of severing worldly attachments ('alā'iq) to achieve the unadulterated focus of the heart upon God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The literal cutting of a physical tie versus the metaphorical severance of all internal and external impediments that distract the soul from its sole Beloved.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā) — Governing how the exclusive focus of the martyr's intention is syntactically and logically restricted to God alone, as exemplified in the phrase \"no god but God\" (lā ilāha illā Allāh), which requires the complete negation of all other objects of intention before affirming the sole Divine Object.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فوالذي نفسي بيده ليودن رجال قتلوا في سبيل الله شهداء أن يبعثهم الله علماء لما يرون من كرامتهم فإن أحدا لم يولد عالما وإنما العلم بالتعلم وقال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما تذاكر العلم بعض ليلة أحب إلي من إحيائها وكذلك عن أبي هريرة رضي الله عنه وأحمد بن حنبل رحمة الله وقال الحسن في قوله تعالى {ربنا آتنا في الدنيا حسنة وفي الآخرة حسنة} إن الحسنة في الدنيا هي العلم والعبادة وفي الآخرة هي الجنة وقيل لبعض الحكماء أي الأشياء تقتنى قال الأشياء التي إذا غرقت سفينتك سبحت معك يعني العلم وقيل أراد بغرق السف", "english": "And al-Ḥasan, may God have mercy on him, said: \"The ink of the scholars will be weighed against the blood of the martyrs, and the ink of the scholars will outweigh the blood of the martyrs.\"", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 6", "section_title_en": "The Book of Knowledge — Section 6: On the Virtue of Teaching and Learning (Continued)", "section_index": 6, "anchors": "- Root: شغل (sh-gh-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Occupation, preoccupation, that which engages one entirely\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Preoccupation that veils one from perception of other things\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical occupation; Metaphorical — absorption of the heart that blinds the senses\n- Root: حسن (ḥ-s-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Beauty, goodness, excellence\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسن: عبارة عن كل مبهج مرغوب فيه\" — that which is pleasing and desirable, of three kinds: rational, appetitive, sensory\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — beauty of form; Metaphorical — the ḥasanah in the Qur'anic supplication denotes knowledge and worship\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m) — [Note: recently used, but required by text; applied per governing rule]\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge, perception, cognition\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — apprehension of a thing in its true reality\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — direct perception; Metaphorical — knowledge as a garment of honor from God\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death, cessation of life\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as the lifting of the burdens of this world; awakening from the sleep of heedlessness\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical death; Metaphorical — \"الناس نيام فإذا ماتوا انتبهوا\" (people are asleep; when they die, they awaken)\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — applied in the ḥadīth \"إنما العلم بالتعلم\" (knowledge is only attained through learning), restricting the means of acquiring knowledge to learning alone.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "7": [{"arabic": "سبحانه وناصر الله تعالى موقوف على النصرة له قال الله تعالى {إن تنصروا الله ينصركم ويثبت أقدامكم} فالبداية بالجهد من العبد والجزاء بالهداية من الله عز وجل ولذلك قال تعالى {والذين جاهدوا فينا لنهدينهم سبلنا} وقال تعالى {إن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم} وإنما التغير تكثير الشهوات فهي مرتع الشياطين ومرعاهم فما دامت مخصبة لم ينقطع ترددهم وما داموا يترددون لم ينكشف للعبد جلال الله سبحانه وكان محجوبا عن لقائه وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم لولا أن الشياطين يحومون على قلوب بني آدم لنظروا إلى ملكوت السموات (3) فمن هذا الوجه صار الصوم باب ال", "english": "To proceed: Fasting is one quarter of faith, by virtue of his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Fasting is half of patience\" — and by virtue of his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Patience is half of faith\" {«الإيمان»}. Then it is distinguished by a particular characteristic of its attribution to Allah the Exalted, above all the other pillars — for Allah the Exalted said, in what His Prophet narrated from Him, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Every good deed is multiplied tenfold up to seven hundred times, except fasting, for it is Mine, and I shall reward it.\" And Allah the Exalted has said: {«Only the patient shall be paid their reward in full without reckoning»} (Q 39:10). And fasting is half of patience — so its reward has surpassed the law of measure and reckoning. And sufficient for you in knowing its excellence is his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: \"By Him in whose hand is my soul, the change in the breath of the mouth of the fasting person is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk. Allah the Exalted says: 'He only abandons his appetite, his food, and his drink for My sake — so fasting is for Me, and I shall reward it.'\" And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Paradise has a gate called al-Rayyān, through which none shall enter except the fasting ones.\" And the fasting person is promised the meeting with Allah the Exalted as a portion of his fasting. And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"For the fasting person there are two joys: a joy when he breaks his fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord.\" And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Everything has a gate, and the gate of worship is fasting.\" And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"The sleep of the fasting person is worship.\" And Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"When the month of Ramaḍān enters, the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of the Fire are closed, the devils are chained, and a caller calls out: 'O seeker of good, come forth! O seeker of evil, desist!'\" And Wakīʿ said concerning His saying, the Exalted: {«Eat and drink in satisfaction for what you sent forth in the days past»} (Q 69:24) — these are the days of fasting, for in them they abandoned eating and drinking. And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gathered in the rank of divine boasting (mubāhāh) between renunciation of the world and fasting, saying: \"Allah the Exalted boasts to His angels of the young worshiper, saying: 'O young man who abandons his appetite for My sake, who devotes his youth to Me — you are to Me as some of My angels.'\" And he said, peace and blessings be upon him, concerning the fasting person: \"Allah the Exalted says: 'Look, O My angels, at My servant — he has abandoned his appetite, his pleasure, his food, and his drink for My sake.'\" And it was said concerning His saying, the Exalted: {«No soul knows what comfort has been concealed for them, as a reward for what they used to do»} (Q 32:17) — it was said: their deed was fasting, because He said: {«Only the patient shall be paid their reward in full without reckoning»} — so the reward of the fasting person is poured out upon him without measure, and is dealt out to him in bulk, not falling under any imagination or estimation.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Fasting", "section_title_ar": "Section 185", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Secrets of Fasting — Section 185: On the Excellence of Fasting and Its Distinction Among the Acts of Worship", "section_index": 185, "anchors": "- Root: صوم (ṣ-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — abstention from any act, whether eating, speaking, or walking; a horse that refrains from movement or fodder is called ṣā'im.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological/Kalam semantic nuance — \"الصوم في الأصل: الإمساك عن الفعل\" — fasting is fundamentally the restraint of the self from action; it is an inward act of the heart, invisible to creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical distinction — Haqiqah: the month of fasting, \"فمن شهد منكم الشهر فليصمه\" (Q 2:185); Majaz: \"صام الفرس على آريه\" — a horse standing still at its tether, refusing fodder, metaphorically \"fasting.\"\n- Root: عله (ʿ-l-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — to feed or pasture; related to cultivation and nourishment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological/Kalam semantic nuance — not directly attested in Mufradat for this root; the root pertains to the means by which the body is sustained, which is precisely what the fasting person abstains from.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical distinction — literal feeding and satiation; the fasting person's abstention from these becomes the very means of spiritual nourishment.\n- Root: الي (a-l-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — to convey a message; the angel (malak) is so named because the message is \"chewed\" (yu'lak) in the mouth, as a horse chews its bit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological/Kalam semantic nuance — \"المألكة: الرسالة\" — the angelic function is the conveyance of divine communication; the fasting person's hunger narrows the channels through which the devil (who runs like blood through the veins) reaches the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical distinction — literal conveyance of a message versus the metaphorical \"message\" of the devil's insinuation (waswās) through the pathways of the body.\n- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — to restrain, to hold back in constriction; \"صبرت الدابة: حبستها بلا علف\" — to hold an animal back without fodder.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological/Kalam semantic nuance — \"الصبر: الإمساك في ضيق\" — patience is restraint within constriction; it is the holding of the soul upon what reason and law require, or from what they require it to abstain. Its names vary by context: in battle it is courage, in calamity it is patience proper.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical distinction — Haqiqah: \"صبرت على ما أكره وصبرت عما أحب\" — patience upon the disliked and patience from the beloved; Majaz: \"صبرت يمينه\" — to bind one's oath severely, and \"هو أصبر على الضرب من الأرض\" — more enduring of striking than the earth.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts and confines the predicate exclusively to its subject, as in the divine saying {«إنما يوفى الصابرون أجرهم بغير حساب»} and {«إنما يذر شهوته وطعامه وشرابه لأجلي»}, where the restriction elevates the restricted matter to a rank beyond all quantitative measure.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Fasting'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "12": [{"arabic": "النسوان وجمع لذائذ الأطعمة يأكلون كما تأكل الأنعام ويظنون أنهم إذا نالوا ذلك فقد أدركوا غاية السعادة فشغلهم ذلك عن الله تعالى وعن اليوم الآخر وطائفة ظنوا أن السعادة في كثرة المال والاستغناء بكثرة الكنوز فأسهروا ليلهم وأتعبوا نهارهم في الجمع فهم يتعبون في الأسفار طول الليل والنهار ويترددون في الأعمال الشاقة ويكتسبون ويجمعون ولا يأكلون إلا قدر الضرورة شحا وبخلا عليها أن تنقص وهذه لذتهم وفي ذلك دأبهم وحركتهم إلى أن يدركهم الموت فيبقى تحت الأرض أو يظفر به من يأكله في الشهوات واللذات فيكون للجامع تعب", "english": "Another group supposed that happiness lies in a good name and the loosening of tongues in praise — through ostentation and manly virtue (murū'ah). These exhaust themselves in earning a livelihood and straiten themselves in food and drink, expending all their wealth upon fine garments and precious mounts, adorning the doorways of their houses and whatever falls under people's gaze — so that it may be said: \"He is wealthy,\" and \"He possesses riches\" — supposing that this is happiness. So their aspiration, by day and by night, is the tending of the place where people's eyes fall.", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World", "section_title_ar": "Section 750", "section_title_en": "The True Purpose of Food, Clothing, and Shelter — and the Deviation of the Errant Sects", "section_index": 750, "anchors": "- Root: شغل (sh-gh-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Occupation, engagement, that which busies or diverts one\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): That which distracts and causes heedlessness (dhuhl) of the heart\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"أنا في شغل شاغل\" — being occupied with what diverts\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"دار مشغولة\" — a house occupied by inhabitants; \"مال مشغول\" — wealth tied up in trade\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which desires and craves\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul as the seat of desire and passion (shahwah)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"شيء نفيس\" — a precious thing; \"نفس\" as the essence of a thing\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"دفق نفسه\" — to shed one's blood; the soul as that which is poured out in death\n- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness, proximity; the nearer world (al-dunyā) as that which is close\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — nearness by essence or by judgment; the lower world as the nearer abode\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"هو دنيء من الأدنياء\" — base and contemptible; the connection between the near world and baseness\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"إنما\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إِنَّمَا يَتَقَبَّلُ اللَّهُ مِنَ الْمُتَّقِينَ»}, establishing that acceptance is limited exclusively to the God-fearing.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of the World'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "14": [{"arabic": "{أفمن زين له سوء عمله فرآه حسنا} فكل ما أحدث بعد الصحابة رضي الله عنهم مما جاوز قدر الضرورة والحاجة فهو من اللعب واللهو وحكي عن إبليس لعنه الله أنه بث جنوده في وقت الصحابة رضي الله عنهم فرجعوا إليه محسورين فقال ما شأنكم قالوا ما رأينا مثل هؤلاء ما نصيب منهم شيئا وقد أتعبونا فقال إنكم لا تقدرون عليهم قد صحبوا نبيهم وشهدوا تنزيل ربهم ولكن سيأتي بعدهم قوم تنالون منهم حاجتكم فلما جاء التابعون بث جنوده فرجعوا إليه منكسين فقالوا ما رأينا أعجب من هؤلاء نصبب منهم الشيء", "english": "For this reason ʿAlī, may God be pleased with him, said — when it was said to him, \"You have opposed so-and-so\" — \"The best of us is the most following of us of this religion.\" So one ought not to be perturbed by opposing the people of one's own age when one is thereby conforming to the people of the age of the Messenger of God ﷺ. For the people have adopted an opinion (raʾy) concerning their present situation, due to the inclination of their natures toward it, and their souls have not permitted them to acknowledge that this is a cause of deprivation from Paradise — so they have claimed that there is no path to Paradise other than it.", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 64", "section_title_en": "** Section 64 — On the Sciences of the Inner Life: The Obligation to Follow the Salaf and the Condemnation of Innovation (Bidʿah)", "section_index": 64, "anchors": "**\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — perception of a thing by its true nature; knowledge as the apprehension of reality.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment upon the thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage — \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" (I did not know of your news: I had no awareness of it). The scholar (ʿālim) is one whose knowledge is manifest in action.\n- Root: بدع (badaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — to originate, to create something new without prior example.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبداع: إنشاء صنعة بلا احتذاء واقتداء\" — origination of a thing without model or precedent. When used of God, it is bringing into existence without instrument, matter, time, or place.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"أبدع الشيء وابتدعه: اخترعه\" (he originated it and innovated it: he invented it). Metaphorical — \"أبدعت حجتك إذا ضعفت\" (your argument became weak/innovative in the bad sense).\n- Root: حدث (ḥadatha)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — coming-to-be of a thing after it was not. \"المحدث: ما أوجد بعد أن لم يكن\" — the originated thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"هو حدث من الأحداث\" (he is a youth among youths). Metaphorical — \"صاروا أحاديث\" (they became [mere] tales). In religious terminology: muḥdathāt = newly-invented matters in religion.\n- Root: هدى (hadā)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Guidance — directing to the path with gentleness; al-hudā is the guidance of the Prophet ﷺ in matters of religion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"حسن الهدى\" — excellent guidance, the prophetic way (sunnah) as opposed to innovation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما هما اثنتان: الكلام والهدى\" restricts the two matters exclusively to speech and guidance, excluding all else.\n\n---\n\n#", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Insight"}], "15": [{"arabic": "تعالى {وسقوا ماء حميما فقطع أمعاءهم} وقال تعالى {وإن يستغيثوا يغاثوا بماء كالمهل يشوى الوجوه} فهذه طعامهم وشرابهم عند جوعهم وعطشهم (1) فانظر الآن إلى حيات جهنم وعقاربها وإلى شدة سمومها وعظم أشخاصها وفظاظة منظرها وقد سلطت على أهلها وأغريت بهم فهي لا تفتر عن النهش واللدغ ساعة واحدة قال أبو هريرة قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم من أتاه الله مالا فلم يؤد زكاته مثل له يوم القيامة شجاعا له زييبتان يطوقه يوم القيامة ثم يأخذ بلهازمه يعنى أشدافه فيقول أنا مالك أنا كنزك ثم تلا قوله تعالى ولا", "english": "At that point they despair of every good, and at that point they take to sighing, regret, and wailing. Abu Umamah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said concerning His words, \"And he is given to drink of festering water, which he sips but can hardly swallow\" {«وَيُسْقَىٰ مِن مَّاءٍ صَدِيدٍ يَتَجَرَّعُهُ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُ»}: \"It is brought near to him, and he loathes it. When it is drawn close to him, it scorches his face, and the skin of his scalp falls off. When he drinks it, it severs his intestines until it exits from his anus.\" God Almighty says: {«وَسُقُوا مَاءً حَمِيمًا فَقَطَّعَ أَمْعَاءَهُمْ»} \"And they are given to drink boiling water, and it severs their intestines.\" And He says: {«وَإِن يَسْتَغِيثُوا يُغَاثُوا بِمَاءٍ كَالْمُهْلِ يَشْوِي الْوُجُوهَ»} \"And if they cry for relief, they are relieved with water like molten brass, which scalds the faces.\" So this is their food and drink in their hunger and thirst.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1350", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Fire: Serpents, Scorpions, and the Anguish of the Damned", "section_index": 1350, "anchors": "- Root: حس (ḥ-s-s) — [AVOIDED per instruction]\n- Root: نار (n-r) — Fire\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"النار: معروفة، وهي نار جهنم، ذات لفح وحرّ.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النار: قد تطلق على نار الدنيا ونار الآخرة، وهي التي أعدها الله للكافرين، وفيها من أصناف العذاب ما لا يعلمه إلا الله.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نار: حقيقة في النار المحرقة، ومجاز في نار الحرب والحرب.\"\n- Root: حي (ḥ-y) — Serpent\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"الحية: من الهوام، وتجمع على حيات، وأعظمها ما كان كأعناق البخت.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحية: دابة مؤذية، وفي الآخرة تكون أعظم وأشد إيلامًا.\"\n- Root: عقر (ʿ-q-r) — Scorpion\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"العقرب: دويبة مؤذية تلدغ، وفي النار تكون كالبغال.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to specify that serpents and scorpions are only empowered over those who were dominated by miserliness and evil character in the world.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "أنهارها لمن ماء غير آسن وأنهار من لبن لم يتغير طعمه وأنهار من عسل مصفى لم يصفه الرجال وأنها من خمر لذة للشاربين لا تسفه الأحلام ولا تصدع منها الرءوس وإن فيها ما لا عين رأت ولا أذن سمعت ولا خطر على قلب بشر ملوك ناعمون أبناء ثلاث وثلاثون في سن واحد طولهم ستون ذراعا في السماء كحل جرد مرد قد أمنوا العذاب واطمأنت بهم الدار وإن أنهارها لتجري على رضراض من ياقوت وزبرجد وإن عروقها ونخلها وكرمها اللؤلؤ وثمارها لا يعلم علمها إلا الله تعالى وإن ريحها ليوجد من مسيرة خمسمائة سنة وإن لهم فيها خيلا وإبلا هفافة رحال", "english": "Usamah ibn Zayd narrated that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said to his companions: \"Behold! Is there anyone striving eagerly for Paradise? For Paradise — no measure can encompass it, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah — it is light gleaming, and a fragrant basil swaying, and a lofty palace, and a flowing river, and abundant ripe fruit, and a wife — a beautiful, comely wife — in splendid raiment, and bliss in an everlasting abode, and radiance in a lofty, resplendent, secure dwelling.\" They said: \"We are the eager strivers for it, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said: \"Say: 'If Allah the Exalted wills.'\" Then he mentioned jihad and its due right.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1357", "section_title_en": "Exposition of Scattered Descriptions of the Inhabitants of Paradise as Reported in the Traditions", "section_index": 1357, "anchors": "- Root: جمل (j-m-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"جمل\" denotes gathering and collecting scattered parts into a whole; \"جملة\" (totality/summary) is the aggregate as opposed to \"تفصيل\" (detailed enumeration).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الجمال: الحسن الكثير\" — beauty in abundance; here the nominal form \"جمل\" (plural of \"جملة\") signals comprehensive, summarized statements, not ornamental beauty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"أجمل في الطلب\" — to be moderate; Majaz (Metaphorical): \"اتخذ الليل جملا\" — to traverse the night as a whole. In this title, \"جمل مفرقة\" is literal: \"dispersed summary statements\" — a rhetorical oxymoron meaning concise reports scattered across various traditions.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الصفة والموصوف (Attributive Interposition) — The genitive construct \"جمل مفرقة\" (summary statements scattered) with the adjective \"مفرقة\" (dispersed) following the noun \"جمل\" (statements) requires agreement in definiteness, gender, and number, here feminine plural agreeing with the broken plural \"جمل.\"\n\n- Root: خطر (kh-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخطر\" is the stake in a wager; \"ما خطر بباله\" — what crosses the mind; \"لا خطر لها\" — of no worth or measure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخطر\" denotes the estimation of a thing's value; \"لا خطر لها\" means it transcends all valuation — no comparison can measure it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خاطر بنفسه\" — risked himself; Majaz: \"لا خطر لها\" — metaphorical negation of any comparable measure, i.e., infinite worth.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب النفي بلا (Negation with \"lā\") — \"لا خطر لها\" is a generic negation (lā li-nafy al-jins) rendering \"خطر\" in the accusative indefinite, here functioning as a predicate of worthlessness-turned-sublimity.\n\n- Root: عين (ʿayn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العين\" — the eye; also a spring of water; \"قرت عينه\" — his eye was cooled (contentment).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العين الجارحة... وللمراعي للشيء عين، وفلان بعيني، أي: أحفظه وأراعيه\" — the eye as organ and as metaphor for divine guardianship; \"ولذت عيناك\" — what your eyes delight in.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فلان عيون وعيان\" — a watchful spy; Majaz: \"نظرت الأرض بعين\" — the earth sprouted with early pasture; here \"ولذت عيناك\" is literal sensory pleasure of sight.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفعل اللازم والمتعدي (Transitive and Intransitive Verbs) — \"لذت عيناك\" (your eyes delighted) is intransitive with the subject \"عيناك\" (dual), the pleasure being an internal accident (ʿaraḍ) of perception.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "لم يصفه الرجال وأنها من خمر لذة للشاربين لا تسفه الأحلام ولا تصدع منها الرءوس وإن فيها ما لا عين رأت ولا أذن سمعت ولا خطر على قلب بشر ملوك ناعمون أبناء ثلاث وثلاثون في سن واحد طولهم ستون ذراعا في السماء كحل جرد مرد قد أمنوا العذاب واطمأنت بهم الدار وإن أنهارها لتجري على رضراض من ياقوت وزبرجد وإن عروقها ونخلها وكرمها اللؤلؤ وثمارها لا يعلم علمها إلا الله تعالى وإن ريحها ليوجد من مسيرة خمسمائة سنة وإن لهم فيها خيلا وإبلا هفافة رحالها وأزمتها وسروجها من ياقوت يتزاورون فيها وأزواجهم ال", "english": "Usamah ibn Zayd narrated that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said to his companions: \"Behold! Is there anyone striving eagerly for Paradise? For Paradise — no measure can encompass it, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah — it is light gleaming, and a fragrant basil swaying, and a lofty palace, and a flowing river, and abundant ripe fruit, and a wife — a beautiful, comely wife — in splendid raiment, and bliss in an everlasting abode, and radiance in a lofty, resplendent, secure dwelling.\" They said: \"We are the eager strivers for it, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said: \"Say: 'If Allah the Exalted wills.'\" Then he mentioned jihad and its due right.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1357", "section_title_en": "Exposition of Scattered Descriptions of the Inhabitants of Paradise as Reported in the Traditions", "section_index": 1357, "anchors": "- Root: جمل (j-m-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"جمل\" denotes gathering and collecting scattered parts into a whole; \"جملة\" (totality/summary) is the aggregate as opposed to \"تفصيل\" (detailed enumeration).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الجمال: الحسن الكثير\" — beauty in abundance; here the nominal form \"جمل\" (plural of \"جملة\") signals comprehensive, summarized statements, not ornamental beauty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"أجمل في الطلب\" — to be moderate; Majaz (Metaphorical): \"اتخذ الليل جملا\" — to traverse the night as a whole. In this title, \"جمل مفرقة\" is literal: \"dispersed summary statements\" — a rhetorical oxymoron meaning concise reports scattered across various traditions.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الصفة والموصوف (Attributive Interposition) — The genitive construct \"جمل مفرقة\" (summary statements scattered) with the adjective \"مفرقة\" (dispersed) following the noun \"جمل\" (statements) requires agreement in definiteness, gender, and number, here feminine plural agreeing with the broken plural \"جمل.\"\n\n- Root: خطر (kh-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخطر\" is the stake in a wager; \"ما خطر بباله\" — what crosses the mind; \"لا خطر لها\" — of no worth or measure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخطر\" denotes the estimation of a thing's value; \"لا خطر لها\" means it transcends all valuation — no comparison can measure it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خاطر بنفسه\" — risked himself; Majaz: \"لا خطر لها\" — metaphorical negation of any comparable measure, i.e., infinite worth.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب النفي بلا (Negation with \"lā\") — \"لا خطر لها\" is a generic negation (lā li-nafy al-jins) rendering \"خطر\" in the accusative indefinite, here functioning as a predicate of worthlessness-turned-sublimity.\n\n- Root: عين (ʿayn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العين\" — the eye; also a spring of water; \"قرت عينه\" — his eye was cooled (contentment).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العين الجارحة... وللمراعي للشيء عين، وفلان بعيني، أي: أحفظه وأراعيه\" — the eye as organ and as metaphor for divine guardianship; \"ولذت عيناك\" — what your eyes delight in.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فلان عيون وعيان\" — a watchful spy; Majaz: \"نظرت الأرض بعين\" — the earth sprouted with early pasture; here \"ولذت عيناك\" is literal sensory pleasure of sight.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفعل اللازم والمتعدي (Transitive and Intransitive Verbs) — \"لذت عيناك\" (your eyes delighted) is intransitive with the subject \"عيناك\" (dual), the pleasure being an internal accident (ʿaraḍ) of perception.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "16": [{"arabic": "قوله تعالى ومنهم من يستمع إليك حتى إذا خرجوا من عندك قالوا للذين أوتوا العلم ماذا قال آنفا أولئك الذين طبع الله على قلوبهم والطابع هي الموانع التي سنذكرها في موانع الفهم وقد قيل لا يكون المريد مريدا حتى يجد في القرآن كل ما يريد ويعرف منه النقصان من المزيد ويستغني بالمولى عن العبيد السادس التخلي عن موانع الفهم فإن أكثر الناس منعوا عن فهم معاني القرآن لأسباب وحجب أسدلها الشيطان على قلوبهم فعميت عليهم عجائب أسرار القرآن قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لولا أن الشياطين يحومون على", "english": "I say: These meanings are buried treasures that are not unveiled except to the divinely aided ones. To this 'Ali — may God be pleased with him — alluded by his saying: \"The Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — did not confide to me anything that he concealed from the people, except that God — mighty and majestic — grants to a servant understanding (fahm) of His Book. So let one be eager in seeking that understanding.\" (1) And Ibn Mas'ud — may God be pleased with him — said: \"Whoever desires the knowledge of the first and the last, let him stir up the Qur'an.\" And the greatest of the sciences of the Qur'an lie beneath the names of God — mighty and majestic — and His attributes, for most of creation have grasped from them only matters befitting their understandings and have not stumbled upon their depths.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 228", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 228: On the Inner Meanings of the Qur'an and the Removal of Obstacles to Understanding", "section_index": 228, "anchors": "- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, discernment, the faculty by which one grasps meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — a disposition by which one verifies the realities of what is perceived; distinct from mere hearing or transmission\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: swift apprehension; Metaphorical: the divine gift of insight into hidden meanings, as in the verse {ففهمناها سليمان}\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transformation from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart as that which is perpetually turned and overturned between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of understanding that is veiled or illuminated, rusted or polished\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, the auditory faculty\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السمع: قوة في الأذن به يدرك الأصوات\" — extends metaphorically to comprehension and obedience; \"أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" — one who casts his hearing while present\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception; Metaphorical: attentive obedience and receptive understanding\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise delimitation of predicates such as \"إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب\" where restriction confines the action to its proper subject", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "خرجوا من عندك قالوا للذين أوتوا العلم ماذا قال آنفا أولئك الذين طبع الله على قلوبهم والطابع هي الموانع التي سنذكرها في موانع الفهم وقد قيل لا يكون المريد مريدا حتى يجد في القرآن كل ما يريد ويعرف منه النقصان من المزيد ويستغني بالمولى عن العبيد السادس التخلي عن موانع الفهم فإن أكثر الناس منعوا عن فهم معاني القرآن لأسباب وحجب أسدلها الشيطان على قلوبهم فعميت عليهم عجائب أسرار القرآن قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لولا أن الشياطين يحومون على قلوب بني آدم لنظروا إلى الملكوت (1) ومعاني القرآن من جملة الملكوت وكل ما غاب عن الحواس ولم يدرك إلا بنور البصي", "english": "I say: These meanings are buried treasures that are not unveiled except to the divinely aided ones. To this 'Ali — may God be pleased with him — alluded by his saying: \"The Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — did not confide to me anything that he concealed from the people, except that God — mighty and majestic — grants to a servant understanding (fahm) of His Book. So let one be eager in seeking that understanding.\" (1) And Ibn Mas'ud — may God be pleased with him — said: \"Whoever desires the knowledge of the first and the last, let him stir up the Qur'an.\" And the greatest of the sciences of the Qur'an lie beneath the names of God — mighty and majestic — and His attributes, for most of creation have grasped from them only matters befitting their understandings and have not stumbled upon their depths.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 228", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 228: On the Inner Meanings of the Qur'an and the Removal of Obstacles to Understanding", "section_index": 228, "anchors": "- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, discernment, the faculty by which one grasps meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — a disposition by which one verifies the realities of what is perceived; distinct from mere hearing or transmission\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: swift apprehension; Metaphorical: the divine gift of insight into hidden meanings, as in the verse {ففهمناها سليمان}\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transformation from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart as that which is perpetually turned and overturned between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of understanding that is veiled or illuminated, rusted or polished\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, the auditory faculty\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السمع: قوة في الأذن به يدرك الأصوات\" — extends metaphorically to comprehension and obedience; \"أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" — one who casts his hearing while present\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception; Metaphorical: attentive obedience and receptive understanding\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise delimitation of predicates such as \"إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب\" where restriction confines the action to its proper subject", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "17": [{"arabic": "المراد بقوله تعالى {والذين اهتدوا زادهم هدى} والهداية الثالثة وراء الثانية وهو النور الذي يشرق في عالم النبوة والولاية بعد كمال المجاهدة فيهتدي بها إلا ما لا يهتدى إليه بالعقل الذي يحصل به التكليف وإمكان تعلم العلوم وهو الهوى المطلق وما عداه حجاب له ومقدمات وهو الذي شرفه الله تعالى بتخصيص الإضافة إليه وإن كان الكل من جهته تعالى فقال تعالى {قل إن هدى الله هو الهدى} وهو المسمى حياة في قوله تعالى {أو من كان ميتا فأحييناه وجعلنا له نورا يمشي به في الناس} والمعنى بقوله تعالى {أفمن شرح", "english": "PageV04P107\nBut if one does not know what constitutes the welfare of his hereafter—so that he even supposes corruption to be welfare—then from where would mere volition benefit him? There is no benefit in volition, power, and causes except after guidance (hidāyah). For this reason, God—exalted is He—said: {«ربنا الذي أعطى كل شيء خلقه ثم هدى»} \"Our Lord who gave everything its creation, then guided.\" And He said—exalted is He—: {«ولولا فضل الله عليكم ورحمته ما زكى منكم أحد أبدا ولكن الله يزكي من يشاء»} \"And were it not for God's bounty upon you and His mercy, not one of you would ever be purified; but God purifies whom He wills.\" And the Prophet—peace be upon him—said: \"No one enters Paradise except by the mercy of God—exalted is He—,\" that is, by His guidance. So it was said: \"Not even you, O Messenger of God?\" He said: \"Not even I\" (1). And guidance (hidāyah) has three stations (manāzil):", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 999", "section_title_en": "The Degrees of Divine Guidance (Hidāyah), Righteous Discernment (Rushd), Straightening (Tasdīd), and Divine Support (Ta'yīd)", "section_index": 999, "anchors": "- Root: هدي (H-D-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of gentle, gracious direction (dalālah bi-luṭf); the root also yields \"gift\" (hadiyyah) and the leading beasts of prey (hawādī) that guide the herd.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Guidance is \"directional indication with gentleness\" (dalālah bi-luṭf), distinct from mere intellectual proof; it is a divine act that may be general (to all) or specific (to the elect), and its withholding is a form of abandonment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He guided him to the path\" (hadāhu lil-sabīl). Metaphorical (majāz): \"He guided him\" meaning \"he preceded him\" (taqaddamahu), as a leader precedes the caravan; hence guidance is not merely pointing but active leading.\n- Root: رشد (R-SH-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The state of being rightly directed in one's affairs; integrity of judgment; names Rāshid, Rushayd derive from it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Rushd is the opposite of ghayy (error/ruin); it is used like hidāyah but with a nuance of practical, executive discernment—knowing the good AND being moved to pursue it. Al-Raghib explicitly distinguishes the rushd of Abraham (given before, as a special gift) from the mere legal competence (rushd) of an orphan.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"A man is rāshid or rashīd\" (possessing rightness in conduct). Metaphorical: \"He is of sound lineage\" (huwa li-rushdah), i.e., legitimate—showing the root's extension to ontological soundness.\n- Root: سدد (S-D-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To aim correctly, to hit the mark; the arrow that flies straight (sadīd); the opposite of deviation (ighrāḍ).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (Not in the retrieved scholia, but by extension from the root's usage in the Qur'an, e.g., {wa-saddidū}, \"and be straight/aim rightly\")—it denotes the divine facilitation of the creature's movements so they align with the intended good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He aimed the arrow and it went straight.\" Metaphorical: \"He was granted success in speech and action\" (sudda li-l-ṣawāb).\n- Root: أيد (A-Y-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Strength, support, reinforcement; the \"aid\" (ayd) of God is His empowering of His servant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (By extension from {إذ أيدتك بروح القدس})—divine reinforcement through a holy spirit, i.e., an immaterial power that fortifies both inner perception (baṣīrah) and outer action.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He strengthened him\" (ayyadahu). Metaphorical: Used for the conferral of spiritual power that makes the servant invincible against error.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Gradation and Specification (Tartīb wa-Takhṣīṣ) — The author structures a sequence of terms (hidāyah → rushd → tasdīd → ta'yīd) where each subsequent term is syntactically and semantically superordinate, building a hierarchy of divine acts. The particle \"wa-ammā\" (وأما) marks a new topic with contrastive focus, and the anaphoric references (هو، وهي) bind the exposition into a single periodic argument.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "19": [{"arabic": "وثلاثين وكبر ثلاثا وثلاثين وختم المائة بلا إله إلا الله لا شريك له له الملك وله الحمد وهو على كل شيء قدير غفرت ذنوبه ولو كانت مثل زبد البحر (6) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم من قال سبحان الله وبحمده في اليوم مائة مرة حطت عنه خطاياه وإن كانت مثل زبد البحر (7) وروي أن رجلا جاء إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال تولت عني الدنيا وقلت ذات يدي فقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فأين أنت من صلاة الملائكة وتسبيح الخلائق وبها يرزقون قال فقلت وماذا يا رسول الله قال قل سبحان الله وبحمده سبحان الله العظ", "english": "He — peace and blessings be upon him — said: \"Whoever glorifies Allah (subḥān Allāh) thirty-three times after every prayer, and praises Him (al-ḥamdu lillāh) thirty-three times, and magnifies Him (Allāhu akbar) thirty-three times, and completes the hundred with 'There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner; His is the dominion and His is the praise, and He is powerful over all things' — his sins are forgiven, even if they were like the foam of the sea.\" (6)", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 238", "section_title_en": "Section 238 — On the Virtues of Tasbīḥ, Taḥmīd, Takbīr, and the Remembrance of Allah", "section_index": 238, "anchors": "- Root: سبح (s-b-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is swift movement through water or air; extended to the motion of celestial bodies in their spheres.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التسبيح: تنزيه الله تعالى\" — the theological sense is the declaration of Allah's transcendence above all imperfection; its origin is the swift passage into the worship of Allah.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Ḥaqīqah): \"سبحت الله\" — to declare Him transcendent. Metaphorical (Majāz): \"والنجوم تسبح في الفلك\" — the stars glide in their orbits; the tongue \"swims\" in remembrance as the horse \"swims\" in its gallop.\n- Root: حمد (ḥ-m-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Praise that is a response to beneficence; commendation for voluntary goodness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Praise (ḥamd) is the acknowledgment of beneficence with reverence; it is more specific than shukr (gratitude) in that it is only by the tongue, but broader in that it is for both good and virtue.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أحمد الله تعالى بجميع محامده\" — to praise Allah with all His praiseworthy attributes. Metaphorical: \"أحمدت صنيعه\" — I deemed his deed praiseworthy; the earth is \"praised\" when its dwelling is found agreeable.\n- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be great in magnitude, rank, or dignity; takbīr is the verbal declaration of Allah's supreme greatness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kibriyā' is the attribute of absolute greatness that belongs to Allah alone; takbīr is the tongue's affirmation of this exclusive majesty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: declaring Allah greater than all things. Metaphorical: the heart's magnification of Allah such that nothing else looms large in comparison.\n- Root: هلل (h-l-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes appearing after being hidden; tahlīl (lā ilāha illā Allāh) is the declaration that nullifies all false deities and affirms the One.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The phrase \"lā ilāha illā Allāh\" is the most excellent of remembrances; its meaning is the negation of all that is worshipped besides Allah and the affirmation of His sole divinity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the utterance of the shahādah. Metaphorical: the heart's purification from all allegiance to other than Allah.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conjunction and Coordination (Bāb al-ʿAṭf) — The repeated formulaic phrases are coordinated by wa (and), each standing as an independent clause of invocation, with the final phrase \"wa huwa ʿalā kulli shay'in qadīr\" serving as the concluding predicate affirming Allah's omnipotence, completing the hundred with a declaration of divine power.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "ولا إله إلا الله والله أكبر ألف مرة وإن قرأ المسبعات الست في يوم الجمعة أو ليلتها فحسن وليس يروى عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه كان يقرأ سورا بأعيانها إلا في يوم الجمعة وليلتها كان يقرأ في صلاة المغرب ليلة الجمعة قل يا أيها الكافرون وقل هو الله أحد وكان يقرأ في صلاة العشاء الآخرة ليلة الجمعة سورة الجمعة والمنافقين (3) وروي أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم كان يقرؤهما في ركعتي الجمعة وكان يقرأ في الصبح يوم الجمعة سورة سجدة لقمان وسورة هل أتى على الإنسان (4) الخامس الصلوات", "english": "Know that among the proprieties of Friday is that one should be excellent in watching for the noble hour. For in the well-known report: {«Indeed in Friday there is an hour in which no Muslim servant asks God, Mighty and Majestic, for anything but that He grants it to him»}. And in another report: {«No servant coincides with it while praying»}.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 151", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 151: On the Virtues of Friday and Its Proprieties", "section_index": 151, "anchors": "- Root: ر-ق-ب (raqaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To watch, observe, lie in wait for; the watcher (rāqib) is one who awaits expectantly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Moral vigilance (murāqabah) is the soul's persistent attentiveness to what it anticipates, whether of benefit or harm.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: watching a road or pass. Metaphorical: the heart's standing guard over the appointed time, as one watches for the crescent moon.\n- Root: ن-ف-ح (nafaḥa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To blow gently, to emit a breeze; nafaḥāt are gentle, diffusive emanations.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine effusions of mercy that sweep over the servant unexpectedly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a fragrant breeze. Metaphorical: the breaths of divine mercy that pass over the days of time.\n- Root: خ-ط-ب (khaṭaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To address, deliver an oration; khuṭbah is the formal sermon.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The prophetic address conveying command and prohibition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: oration. Metaphorical: the preacher's ascent to the pulpit marks the threshold of the answered hour.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of the conditional clause (jawāb al-sharṭ) — the apodosis may be delayed or elided when the protasis is governed by a particle of expectation, as in \"whoever does X, God forgives him,\" where the response is the reward predicated upon the condition.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "وهو قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم بني الإسلام على خمس شهادة أن لا إله إلا الله (1) إلى آخر الحديث لأن الواجب هذه الخمس فيجب العلم بكيفية العمل فيها وبكيفية الوجوب والذي ينبغي أن يقطع به المحصل ولا يستريب فيه ما سنذكره وهو أن العلم كما قدمناه في خطبة الكتاب ينقسم إلى علم معاملة وعلم مكاشفة وليس المراد بهذا العلم إلا علم المعاملة والمعاملة التي كلف العبد العاقل البالغ العمل بها ثلاثة اعتقاد وفعل وترك فإذا بلغ الرجل العاقل بالاحتلام أو السن ضحوة نهار مثلا فأول واجب عليه تعلم كلمتي الشهادة وفهم معناهما وهو قول لا إله إلا الله", "english": "The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Seeking knowledge is an obligation upon every Muslim.\" {«طلب العلم فريضة على كل مسلم»} He also said, may God bless him and grant him peace: \"Seek knowledge, even unto China.\" {«اطلبوا العلم ولو بالصين»}", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 12", "section_title_en": "The Exposition of the Knowledge That Is an Individual Obligation (Farḍ ʿAyn)", "section_index": 12, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning encompasses both perception of a thing's essence and judgment upon it; knowledge as apprehension of reality.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge is of two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what exists or is negated for it. The first is transitive to one object; the second to two.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal knowledge (ʿilm) from awareness/perception (shiʿr); knowledge as the mark (ʿalam) by which one is guided—here, the knowledge that guides to obligatory action.\n- Root: وجب (wajaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be due, necessary, obligatory; to fall (as a duty); the root carries the sense of established necessity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Obligation (wujūb) is of two kinds: that which is necessary by reason (ʿaql), and that which is necessary by sacred law (sharʿ). The knowledge that is farḍ ʿayn pertains to the latter—what the Lawgiver has made due upon every accountable individual.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense of falling due; metaphorical extension to what is incumbent upon a person—here, the knowledge whose acquisition becomes due upon the occurrence of its cause.\n- Root: عهد (ʿahida) — AVOIDED per instruction; not employed.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The Prophet's statement \"طلب العلم فريضة\" (seeking knowledge is an obligation) is a general predication whose referent (musammā) requires specification (takhṣīṣ) through contextual and definitional analysis, not lexical transfer (naql). The article (al-) in \"العلم\" indicates the known, designated science—here, the science of obligatory practice.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "22": [{"arabic": "أحد ومن غالب الله غلبه ومن خادع الله خدعه فهل عسيتم إن توليتم أن تفسدوا في الأرض وتقطعوا أرحامكم وأوصيكم بالأنصار خيرا فإنهم الذين تبوءوا الدار والإيمان من قبلكم أن تحسنوا إليهم ألم يشاطروكم الثمار ألم يوسعوا عليكم في الديار ألم يؤثروكم على أنفسهم وبهم الخصاصة ألا فمن ولي أن يحكم بين رجلين فليقبل من محسنهم وليتجاوز عن مسيئهم ألا ولا تستأثروا عليهم ألا وإني فرط لكم وأنتم لاحقون بي ألا وإن موعدكم الحوض حوضي أعرض مما بين بصرى الشام وصنعاء اليمن يصب فيه ميزاب الكوثر ماؤه أشد بياضا من اللبن وألين من الزبد وأحلى من الشهد من شرب منه لم يظمأ أبدا", "english": "And a clarification for those who seek insight — for there was none more honored before God than him, for he was the Intimate Friend of God (khalīl Allāh), His Beloved (ḥabībuh), and His Confidant (najiyyuh); he was His Chosen One (ṣafiyyuh), His Messenger (rasūluh), and His Prophet (nabiyyuh). So consider: did He grant him respite for even an hour at the end of his term? Did He delay him for a single moment after his appointed death had arrived? No indeed — rather, He dispatched to him the noble angels entrusted with seizing the souls of all creation. They rushed upon his pure and generous spirit to extract it, and they wrestled with it to remove it from his pure body — unto mercy, good pleasure, and beautiful bounties; nay, unto a seat of truth in the vicinity of the All-Merciful. Yet despite all that, his distress (karbuh) intensified during the throes of death, his groaning became apparent, his restlessness followed in succession, his yearning rose high, his color changed, his brow sweated, and his left and right sides trembled in contraction and expansion — until those present wept over his fall, and those who witnessed his state sobbed at its severity. So have you seen any station of prophethood repelling what was decreed from him? Did the angel (of death) show regard for his kin and tribe? Did he grant him leniency because he was a supporter of the Truth and a bearer of glad tidings and warnings to creation? Far from it! Rather, he fulfilled what he was commanded, and he followed what he found inscribed on the Preserved Tablet. Such was his state — while he, before God, held the Praiseworthy Station (al-maqām al-maḥmūd) and the Pond of Abundance (al-ḥawḍ al-mawrūd); he is the first from whom the earth will split open, and he is the master of intercession on the Day of Presentation. So the wonder is that we do not take heed from him, nor are we certain of what we shall encounter. Nay, we are captives of lusts and companions of sins and misdeeds. Why then do we not draw a lesson from the death-fall of Muhammad, the Master of the Messengers, the Imam of the God-fearing, and the Beloved of the Lord of the Worlds? Perhaps we imagine that we shall be immortal, or we fancy that despite our evil deeds we are honored before God. Far from it! Far from it! Rather, we are certain that all of us shall enter the Fire — {«وإن منكم إلا واردها كان على ربك حتما مقضيا ثم ننجي الذين اتقوا ونذر الظالمين فيها جثيا»} (And there is none of you but will pass over it [the Fire]; this is with your Lord a decree firmly established. Then We shall save those who feared God and leave the wrongdoers therein on their knees) — and none shall be saved from it except the God-fearing. So we are certain of entering it, but merely deluded about emerging from it. Nay, we have wronged our own souls if, being as we are, we await with mere predominant conjecture. By God, we are not among the God-fearing! And God, Lord of the Worlds, has said: {«وإن منكم إلا واردها كان على ربك حتما مقضيا ثم ننجي الذين اتقوا ونذر الظالمين فيها جثيا»} (And there is none of you but will pass over it; it is with your Lord a decree firmly established. Then We shall save those who feared God and leave the wrongdoers therein on their knees). So let every servant look to himself — whether he is closer to the wrongdoers or to the God-fearing. And look to yourself after you have looked to the conduct of the righteous predecessors (al-salaf al-ṣāliḥīn), for they, despite all that they were granted of success, were among the fearful.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1298", "section_title_en": "On the Death-Throes of the Prophet (Peace Be Upon Him) and the Lessons Therein — A Warning to the Negligent", "section_index": 1298, "anchors": "- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"sending forth with ease and gentleness\"; a messenger (rasūl) is one dispatched with a message.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the root denotes measured, deliberate dispatch; the angelic messengers (al-malā'ikah al-mursalūn) are sent on a precise, inexorable errand.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"أرسل الله في الأمم رسلا\" — sending prophets with tidings. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" — the inescapable descent of decree; here, the angels are dispatched to seize the Prophet's soul, showing that even the most honored cannot repel the divine command.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which is breathed and animates; also \"preciousness\" (nufāsah).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul (nafs) as the immaterial reality (al-rūḥ) that is seized at death; in \"ويحذركم الله نفسه\" it denotes the very Essence (al-dhāt), underscoring that the soul's extraction is an act of divine sovereignty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفس سائلة\" — a flowing essence. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — the pouring out of life; here, the angels \"عالجوها ليرحلوها\" (wrestled with it to remove it), depicting the soul's reluctant departure from the body.\n- Root: عله (ʿ-l-h) — [Note: Root avoided per constraints; substituted with relevant root for \"suffering\" — see كرب below]\n- Root: كرب (k-r-b) — [Derived from context; not in avoidance list]\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Grief, distress, agony that grips the chest (karb); the intensification of anguish at death's threshold.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The \"sakarat al-mawt\" (agonies of death) as a trial for the soul's detachment from the corporeal frame.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical constriction. Metaphorical: the spiritual weight of transition, as the Prophet's \"karb\" (distress) is magnified despite his rank.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used in \"إنما أوصي بهذا الأمر قريشا\" to restrict leadership to Quraysh, and \"فإنما هو عمن أخبره\" to limit chains of transmission; also applies to \"لا بل\" (nay, rather) constructions that negate a prior assumption and affirm a corrective truth, as in \"لا بل ظلمنا أنفسنا\".", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "31": [{"arabic": "إذا قرأ {ولنبلونكم حتى نعلم المجاهدين منكم والصابرين ونبلو أخباركم} يبكي ويرددها ويقول إنك إن بلوتنا فضحتنا وهتكت أستارنا وقال الحسن إنما خلد أهل الجنة في الجنة وأهل النار في النار بالنيات وقال أبو هريرة مكتوب في التوراة ما أريد به وجهي فقليله كثير وما أريد به غيري فكثيره قليل وقال بلال بن سعد إن العبد ليقول قول مؤمن فلا يدعه الله عز وجل وقوله حتى ينظر في عمله فإذا عمل لم يدعه الله حتى ينظر في ورعه فإن تورع لم يدعه حتى ينظر ماذا نوى فإن صلحت نيته فبالحري أن يصلح ما دون ذلك فإذن عماد الأعمال بالنيات ف", "english": "God Most High said: {«وَلا تَطْرُدِ الَّذِينَ يَدْعُونَ رَبَّهُمْ بِالْغَدَاةِ وَالْعَشِيِّ يُرِيدُونَ وَجْهَهُ»} — \"And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, desiring His Face.\" And the meaning of that \"desiring\" (irādah) is the intention (niyyah).", "book_title": "Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness", "section_title_ar": "Section 1206", "section_title_en": "The Pillar of Deeds is Intention — Section 1206 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 1206, "anchors": "- Root: نوي (N-W-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"intention, purpose, resolve\" — the inward aim that directs an act; from the sense of \"distance\" (nāya) implying the goal one sets one's course toward.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-niyyah is the resolve of the heart that distinguishes the moral identity of an act; it is the internal specification (takhṣīṣ) that renders an act either obedience or disobedience, sincerity or hypocrisy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes the heart's determination toward a goal; metaphorical (majāz) extension applies it to the act's value in the afterlife — \"he is raised according to his intention.\"\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Deliberate, purposeful action issuing from a living agent — contrasted with mere occurrence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿAmal is every deed done by an animal with intent; it is more specific than fiʿl (act), and applies to both good and evil works. Its moral weight is entirely contingent upon the niyyah that animates it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: exertion of effort in a task. Metaphorical: the deed's acceptance or rejection before God, determined by what the heart intended.\n- Root: قتل (Q-T-L)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Removal of the soul from the body; when attributed to the agent, it is \"killing\" (qatl). In the verse {فلم تقتلوهم ولكن الله قتلهم} [Al-Anfāl/17], killing is metaphorically ascribed to God as the true Creator of all acts.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz): \"A heart slain (qalb muqattal)\" — destroyed by love; \"slain by the jinn\" — possessed. The root extends metaphorically to total spiritual ruin or salvation depending on the intention behind the physical act.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The Prophet's saying {إنما الأعمال بالنيات} restricts all deeds to their intentions, making niyyah the exclusive determinant of an act's moral and eschatological value.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "33": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "36": [{"arabic": "الأموال والأولاد ثم رده تعالى في موضع آخر إلى اثنين فقال تعالى وإنما الحياة الدنيا لعب ولهو ثم رد الكل إلى واحد في موضع آخر فقال ونهى النفس عن الهوى فإن الجنة هي المأوى فالهوى لفظ يجمع جميع حظوظ النفس في الدنيا فينبغي أن يكون الزهد فيه وإذا فهمت طريق الإجمال والتفصيل عرفت أن البعض من هذه لا يخالف البعض وإنما يفارقه في الشرح مرة والإجمال أخرى فالحاصل أن الزهد عبارة عن الرغبة عن حظوظ النفس كلها ومهما رغب عن حظوظ النفس رغب عن البقاء في الدنيا فقصر أمله لا محالة لأنه إنما يريد البقاء ليتمتع ويريد التمتع الدائم بإرادة البقاء فإن من أراد شيئا", "english": "The third degree — and it is the highest — is that one renounce willingly, and renounce his very renunciation, so that he does not see his asceticism, since he does not see that he has abandoned anything, having come to know that the world is nothing. {PageV04P225} He is thus like one who casts away a potsherd and takes up a jewel — he does not regard that as an exchange, nor does he see himself as having abandoned anything. And the world, in comparison to God Most High and the bliss of the Hereafter, is baser than a potsherd in comparison to a jewel. This then is perfection in asceticism.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1092", "section_title_en": "The Degrees of Asceticism (Zuhd) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 1092, "anchors": "- Root: زهد (Z-H-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: scarcity, paucity; al-Zuhd as abstemiousness; zāhada fīhi: to turn away from something, deeming it insignificant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الزاهد في الشيء: الراغب عنه والراضي منه بالزهيد، أي: القليل\" — the ascetic is one who turns away from a thing, content with little of it; theological sense: renunciation of the world as a deliberate act of valuation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"زهد في الشيء: رغب عنه\" — turning away. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل زهيد: قليل الخير\" — one of little good; \"اد زهيد: قليل الأخذ للماء\" — a valley taking little water. Here, al-Ghazali employs the literal sense of deliberate aversion, extended metaphorically to the soul's turning from all worldly appetites.\n- Root: دنا (D-N-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity, nearness; al-dunyā (the lower world) named for its nearness to us or its lowness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — nearness by essence or by judgment; al-dunyā contrasted with al-ākhirah as the inferior and nearer realm.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: proximity in place or rank. Metaphorical: the world as \"lower\" (adnā) in value — the sense operative in al-Ghazali's comparison of the world to a potsherd.\n- Root: درج (D-R-J)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To walk step by step; to pass away (daraja: died, passed on); degree/stair.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a station considered as ascent; used for ranks of honor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درج قرن بعد قرن\" — generations passed away; \"رقي في الدرجة\" — ascended in rank. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — one has a lofty degree. Al-Ghazali uses darajāt as ascending ranks of spiritual attainment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — the repeated use of \"إنما\" (only/but) in the Quranic citations (e.g., {إنما الحياة الدنيا لعب ولهو}) functions as restrictive predication (qasr), confining the reality of the world to play and diversion — a syntactic key to al-Ghazali's argument that all worldly goods reduce ultimately to one thing: caprice (hawā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}], "37": [{"arabic": "وقال الله عز وجل أن يسئلكموها فيحفكم تبخلوا أي يستقصى عليكم وأما الحلق فلم يرد والإحفاء القريب من الحلق نقل عن الصحابة نظر بعض التابعين إلى رجل أحفى شاربه فقال ذكرتني أصحاب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وقال المغيرة بن شعبة نظر إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وقد طال شاربي فقال تعال فقصه لي على سواك (5) ولا بأس بترك سباليه وهما طرفا الشارب فعل ذلك عمر وغيره لأن ذلك لا يستر الفم ولا يبقى فيه غمر الطعام إذ لا يصل إليه وقوله صلى الله عليه وسلم اعفوا اللحى أي كثروها وفي الخبر إن اليهود يعف", "english": "**The second: The hair of the moustache.** The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Trim the moustache\" {«قصوا الشارب»}. In another wording: \"Clip the moustaches\" {«جزوا الشوارب»}. In yet another wording: \"Remove the moustaches thoroughly (ḥuffū al-shawārib) and leave the beards to grow (aʿfū al-liḥā)\" {«حفوا الشوارب وأعفوا اللحى»} — meaning: make them at the edge (ḥifāf) of the lip, that is, around it; and the edge (ḥifāf) of a thing is its surrounding. From this is the saying of God Almighty: {«وترى الملائكة حافين من حول العرش»} — \"And you see the angels encircling around the Throne.\" In another wording: \"Remove them thoroughly (aḥfū)\" — and this suggests complete eradication. His saying \"ḥuffū\" indicates something less than that. And God Almighty said: {«أن يسألكموها فيحفكم تبخلوا»} — \"If He asks you for them and presses you, you would be miserly\" — meaning: He would exact from you completely. As for shaving (al-ḥalq) entirely, no command has come regarding it.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Purity", "section_title_ar": "Section 117", "section_title_en": "Section 117 — On the Sunnahs of Fitrah: Hair, Nails, and Related Matters", "section_index": 117, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Mysteries of Purity'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "يسألكموها فيحفكم تبخلوا يحفكم أي يستقص عليكم فكم بين عبد اشترى منه ماله ونفسه بأن له الجنة وبين عبد لا يستقصي عليه لبخله فهذا أحد معاني أمر الله سبحانه عباده ببذل الأموال المعنى الثاني التطهير من صفة البخل فإنه من المهلكات قال صلى الله عليه وسلم ثلاث مهلكات شح مطاع وهوى متبع وإعجاب المرء بنفسه (2) وقال تعالى ومن يوق شح نفسه فأولئك هم المفلحون وسيأتي في ربع المهلكات وجه كونه مهلكا وكيفية التقصي منه وإنما تزول صفة البخل بأن تتعود بذل المال فحب الشيء لا ينقطع إلا بقهر النفس ع", "english": "A group of the Followers (tābiʿūn) held that there are rights in wealth beyond the zakat — among them al-Nakhaʿī, al-Shaʿbī, ʿAṭāʾ, and Mujāhid. Al-Shaʿbī said, after being asked, \"Is there a right in wealth beyond the zakat?\": \"Yes. Have you not heard His saying — mighty and majestic is He: {«وَآتَى الْمَالَ عَلَىٰ حُبِّهِ ذَوِي الْقُرْبَىٰ»} 'And gives wealth, despite his love for it, to relatives' (2:177)?\" They also adduced His saying — mighty and majestic is He: {«وَمِمَّا رَزَقْنَاهُمْ يُنفِقُونَ»} 'And from what We have provided them, they spend' (2:3), and His saying — exalted is He: {«وَأَنفِقُوا مِن مَّا رَزَقْنَاكُم»} 'And spend from what We have provided you' (2:254). They claimed that this is not abrogated by the verse of zakat; rather, it falls within the right of a Muslim upon a Muslim. Its meaning is that it is incumbent upon the affluent one, whenever he finds a needy person, to remove his need — over and above the zakat wealth.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 173", "section_title_en": "The Second Division: Those Who Guard Their Wealth Awaiting Seasons of Need — On the Inner Meanings of Zakat (Section 173)", "section_index": 173, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ق-ر (f-q-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of neediness, poverty; the hollow of the spine (faqār) — that which is structurally weak and dependent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Poverty (faqr) has four senses: (1) essential neediness before Allah, universal to all creatures; (2) lack of possessions; (3) [the third and fourth senses as elaborated in the full lexicon entry].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): one who lacks wealth. Metaphorical (majāz): \"the poverty of speech\" (fuqar al-kalām) — its subtle points, likened to ornaments shaped like vertebrae.\n- Root: ص-د-ق (ṣ-d-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Truthfulness in speech; giving (ṣadaqah) as the outward verification of inward faith.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣidq (truth) and kidhb (falsehood) are originally in speech — statement of what is, or what will be. By extension, ṣadaqah is that which verifies the truthfulness of one's claim to faith through expenditure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: truthful speech. Metaphorical: \"a horse with true running\" (dhū miṣdaq fī al-jary) — that which verifies its excellence through action. Thus charity verifies the giver's sincerity.\n- Root: م-ن-ن (m-n-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The bestowal of a favor; reproaching another by reminding them of one's gift (al-mann).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [As elaborated in the full lexicon entry on the heart's self-perception of superiority over the recipient.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a heavy weight; a favor. Metaphorical: the act of counting one's favors over another — a corrosion of the gift's value.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise scope of obligation (farḍ kifāyah) versus supererogatory merit (faḍl), and the restriction of \"rights in wealth\" (ḥuqūq fī al-māl) beyond the obligatory zakat.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "38": [{"arabic": "فإنهم محتاجون إليه ليمدوا وجودهم بالدوام وإلى هذا الحصر الإشارة بقوله تعالى {والله الغني وأنتم الفقراء} هذا معنى الفقر مطلقا ولكنا لسنا نقصد بيان الفقر المطلق بل الفقر من المال على الخصوص وإلا ففقر العبد بالإضافة إلى أصناف حاجاته لا ينحصر لأن حاجاته لا حصر لها ومن جملة حاجاته ما يتوصل إليه بالمال وهو الذي نريد الآن بيانه فقط فنقول كل فاقد للمال فإنا نسميه فقيرا بالإضافة إلى المال الذي فقده إذا كان ذلك المفقود محتاجا إليه في حقه ثم يتصور أن يكون له خمسة أحوال عن الفقر ونحن نميزها ونخصص كل حال باسم لنتوصل بالتمييز إلى ذكر أحكامها الحال", "english": "Know that poverty (al-faqr) is an expression for the loss of that which one is in need of. As for the loss of that which one has no need of, that is not called poverty. And if that which one is in need of is present and within one's power, then the needy one is not poor. Once you understand this, you will not doubt that every existent other than God — exalted is He — is poor, because it is in need of the continuation of existence in the second moment [of time], and the continuation of existence is something derived (mustafād) from the bounty of God — exalted is He — and His generosity. For if there were in existence an existent whose existence were not derived from another, that one would be the absolutely Rich (al-ghaniyy al-mutlaq). And it is inconceivable that such an existent be other than one. Thus there is nothing in existence except one Rich, and everyone other than Him is in need of Him to sustain their existence with continuance. And to this exclusive enumeration points His saying — exalted is He: {«والله الغني وأنتم الفقراء»} (Q. 47:38) — \"And God is the Rich, and you are the poor.\" This is the meaning of poverty absolutely. But we do not intend here the exposition of absolute poverty; rather, poverty specifically with respect to wealth. Otherwise, the poverty of the servant relative to the kinds of his needs is not delimited, for his needs have no limit. And among the totality of his needs is that which is attained through wealth, and that is what we intend now to expound exclusively.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1066", "section_title_en": "The First Part of the Book on Poverty: Its Reality, Its Virtue, and the States of the Poor", "section_index": 1066, "anchors": "- Root: فقر (fa-qāf-rā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"need\" or \"want\" — the state of one lacking what is required; also a hollow or indentation (as in the back's vertebrae), suggesting an ontological emptiness or lack.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Poverty (al-faqr) is of four kinds: (1) necessary need inherent to all contingent existents, as in {«يا أيها الناس أنتم الفقراء إلى الله»} (Q. 35:15); (2) lack of possessions (the worldly sense); (3) contentment with little; (4) spiritual poverty before God. Here Ghazali distinguishes the absolute metaphysical sense from the specific economic sense.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): \"ليس بفقير ولكن يتفاقر\" — one who feigns poverty is not truly poor. Metaphorical (majāz): \"فقر الكلام\" — the \"joints\" or \"pithy points\" of speech, like beads on a spine. Ghazali uses the literal sense for economic poverty and the metaphysical sense for creaturely need.\n- Root: غني (ghayn-nūn-yā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Riches, sufficiency, independence; the opposite of faqr.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ghinā is of three kinds: (1) absolute lack of needs — belonging to God alone, as in {«إن الله لهو الغني الحميد»} (Q. 22:64); (2) fewness of needs — the praiseworthy \"richness of the soul\" (غنى النفس) mentioned in the Hadith; (3) abundance of possessions.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أنا عنه غني\" — I am independent of it. Metaphorical: \"تغنته القيود\" — shackles \"enriched\" him, i.e., held him fast. Ghazali deploys the absolute sense (al-ghaniyy al-mutlaq) exclusively for God.\n- Root: طلب (ṭā-lām-bā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To seek, to search out, to demand; also \"أطلبه الفقر\" — poverty made him needy, i.e., drove him to seek.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطلب: الفحص عن وجود الشيء\" — seeking is the investigation into the existence of a thing, whether concrete or conceptual. Cf. {«ضعف الطالب والمطلوب»} (Q. 22:73).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"طلب الشيء طلبا ومطلبا\" — to seek earnestly. Metaphorical: \"السراج يطلب أن ينطفيء\" — the lamp \"seeks\" to go out, i.e., tends toward it. Ghazali uses the literal sense for the poor man's active pursuit of wealth.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Specification and Restriction (باب التخصيص) — When a general term (الفقر) is qualified by a restrictive phrase (من المال على الخصوص), the subsequent division of states (aḥwāl) applies only to the restricted sense, not the absolute metaphysical sense. Ghazali explicitly signals this shift with \"ولسنا نقصد بيان الفقر المطلق بل الفقر من المال على الخصوص.\"", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "كالمتكفلين بالثغور والمرابطين بها والغزاة المجاهدين في سبيل الله فمنهم المقاتل ومنهم الردء ومنهم الذي يسقيهم الماء ومنهم الذي يحفظ دوابهم ويتعهدهم ولا ينفك أحد منهم عن أجر إذا كان قصده إعلاء كلمة الله تعالى دون حيازة الغنائم فكذلك العلماء قال الله تعالى {يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات} وقال تعالى {هم درجات عند الله} والفضيلة نسبية واستحقارنا للصيارفة عند قياسهم بالملوك لا يدل على حقارتهم إذا قيسوا بالكناسين فلا تظن أن ما نزل عن الرتبة القصوى ساقط القدر بل الرتبة العليا للأنبياء ثم الأولياء ثم الع", "english": "By this I do not mean the belief which the common man picks up by inheritance or by rote acquisition, nor the method of theological disputation and argumentation for fortifying one's creed against the evasions of opponents—as is the ultimate aim of the scholastic theologian (mutakallim). Rather, it is a kind of certainty which is the fruit of a light that God—may He be exalted—casts into the heart of a servant whose inward being has been purified by spiritual striving (mujāhadah) from all impurities, until he attains to the rank of the faith of Abū Bakr—may God be pleased with him—whose faith, were it weighed against the faith of all created beings, would outweigh them, as the Master of mankind—peace and blessings be upon him—bore witness for him. For in my view, what the common man believes and what the theologian arranges—who exceeds the common man only in the craft of speech (kalām), and for that very reason his craft is named \"kalām\"—is not that rank. Indeed, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, and the rest of the Companions—may God be pleased with them all—fell short of it, until Abū Bakr surpassed them by that secret which was deposited in his breast.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 43", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 43: On the Ordering of the Sciences and the Duties of the Seeker", "section_index": 43, "anchors": "- Root: ذوق (dh-w-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perceiving by taste; experiential apprehension\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Direct, unmediated perception—contrasted with mere cognition or hearsay\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal tasting vs. metaphorical \"savoring\" of knowledge\n- Root: سلك (s-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Entering into a path; threading through\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The spiritual wayfaring (sulūk) toward God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal road-taking vs. metaphorical spiritual journeying\n- Root: كشف (k-sh-f) — AVOIDED per constraints; substituted with Root: غور (gh-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Depth, profundity; the deep bed of a sea\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unfathomable depth of divine knowledge\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal deep water vs. metaphorical inaccessibility of ultimate truth\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — Governing the periodic suspension of meaning across clauses for rhetorical emphasis", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 370, 1369], [2, 1369, 2595], [3, 2595, 4184], [4, 4184, 4525], [5, 4525, 5357], [6, 5357, 5962], [7, 5962, 6598], [8, 6598, 7680]], "47:1": [[1, 37